/// # The tensor Plugin {#tensor}
///
/// @see [`-X tensor:<arg>`](@ref xarg_tensor)
/// @see mim::plug::tensor
///
/// [TOC]
///
/// SSA-world tensor operations: a generic `map_reduce` loop-nest abstraction plus derived operations
/// (reshape, slice, convolution, pooling, dot product, ...), lowered via `affine` loops and bufferized
/// onto the `buffer` layer.
///
/// ## Dependencies
///
plugin tuple;
plugin refly;
plugin core;
import compile;
plugin option;
plugin vec;
plugin cps;
plugin affine;
plugin buffer;
plugin btensor;
plugin math;

use core.ops.n;
///
/// ## Types
///
/// ### Ring
///
/// Represents an [algebraic ring](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ring_(mathematics)).
/// E.g., `let nat_ring = (Nat, 0, core.nat.add, core.nat.mul);`.
///
/// An alias for `btensor.Ring`.
/// The buffer-world `btensor` plugin sits *below* `tensor` — `lower_to_mem` bufferizes onto `btensor.*` ops -
/// so the definition lives there and both layers share one notion of a ring.
anx Ring = btensor.Ring;
///
/// ## Constructors
///
/// Build a tensor from nothing but its shape.
///
/// ### generate
///
/// Constructs a tensor by evaluating `body` once for every logical output coordinate.
/// Coordinates use `I64` so the body can do ordinary scalar arithmetic before converting them to bounded tensor indices.
///
/// ```python
/// output = empty(s_out)
/// for index in ndindex(s_out):
///     output[index] = body(index)
/// return output
/// ```
///
axm generate: {T: *, r: Nat}
            → [s_out: «r; Nat»]
            → [body: [«r; I64»] → T]
            → «s_out; T»;
///
/// ### splat
///
/// Constructs a tensor whose every element is `value`.
/// Keeping this explicit lets bufferization pick a fill operation instead of first building an index
/// lambda or a monolithic nested-array literal.
///
/// ```python
/// output = empty(s_out)
/// for index in ndindex(s_out):
///     output[index] = value
/// return output
/// ```
axm splat: {T: *, r: Nat}
         → [s_out: «r; Nat», value: T]
         → «s_out; T»;
///
/// ## Annotations
///
/// ### buf
///
/// Nominates an array value as a *tensor*: one that lives in a buffer and is passed by
/// handle, rather than an SSA aggregate that merely shares the `Arr` structure - an `(x y: I32)` group *is*
/// a `«2; I32»`.
/// The result type is the argument's, so an annotated tensor extracts, inserts and folds exactly like any
/// other array and no tensor operation has to reimplement what MimIR's arrays already do.
///
/// Annotate *boundary* values only - an external function's array parameters.
/// Everything a tensor operation produces is self-identifying, and `lower_to_mem` propagates tensor-ness
/// from there; it consumes the annotation and every array type it reaches becomes a `buffer.Buf`.
/// Losing one before that silently leaves a tensor with value semantics.
axm buf: {T: *} → T → T;
///
/// ### barrier
///
/// The identity, except that `fuse_tensor` does not fuse across it: the producer of its argument stays a
/// materialized tensor, and its consumers read that tensor instead of recomputing it through their
/// access maps.
/// This keeps a layout transform - e.g. an operand packed for a contraction - which read-through fusion
/// would otherwise absorb again.
/// `lower_to_mem` and `lower_map_reduce` drop it.
axm barrier: {T: *} → T → T, normalize_barrier;
///
/// ### compute_at
///
/// The identity on a map_reduce operand, except that its producer runs inside the consumer's loop nest (TVM's
/// `compute_at`): once per value of the consumer's leading `level` parallel dims, over just the region the loops below
/// them read, into a reused tile.
/// `fuse_tensor` does not fuse across it.
/// The tile needs the operand to be the sole use of a `map_reduce` whose output is not packed, read through an access
/// map that is affine in the loop vector with non-negative coefficients. At level zero a non-linear read map
/// instead builds the whole producer once before the consumer's loops. Otherwise it is materialized as usual.
/// Either way the result is the same.
axm compute_at: {T: *} → [level: Nat] → T → T;
///
/// ## Accessors
///
/// Element access is a plain `Extract` / `Insert`: `arr#(a, b, c)` ≡ `arr#a#b#c`.
///
/// ### shape
///
/// Returns the shape of `arr` as a rank-`r` array of Nats: `(s#0, …, s#(r-1))`.
///
/// The rank `r` is explicit: it selects the first `r` axes of `arr`'s fused shape.
/// Size-1 axes collapse out of the type, so `arr` must have no literal-1 dims among the first `r`.
///
axm shape: {A: *} → [r: Nat] → [arr: A] → «r; Nat», normalize_shape;
///
/// ## Map-Reduce
///
/// The loop-nest core every other operation is lowered onto: `lower_tensor` re-applies the matching
/// annex from Implementations, while `broadcast`, `concat`, `gather`, `pad` and `scatter` are expanded
/// by `lower_map_reduce` directly.
///
/// ### id
///
/// The CPS identity (with an empty epilogue-input pack) — the neutral `post` epilogue.
anx fun id {T: *} (x: T, _: [])@tt: T = return x;
///
/// ### map_reduce_post
///
/// The workhorse behind every tensor operation: a reduction fold over an affinely-indexed loop nest.
/// The loop vector `(o…, r…)` has length `Rn`; its leading `Ro` dims are the parallel output loops,
/// the trailing ones the reduction loops that are folded away.
///
/// - `Sr`, `So`: the full loop bounds and the result shape (rank `Ro`).
///   `So` may differ from the leading `Ro` bounds of `Sr` — that is what lets `map_out` write to a
///   re-shaped output, e.g. a transpose.
/// - `f`: folds one element of each input into the accumulator, seeded per output cell with `init`.
/// - `post`: maps the folded accumulator, plus one element of each of the `nps` *epilogue inputs*
///   `post_is`, to the stored element.
///   It runs once per output cell, between fold and write-back — the slot `fuse_tensor` composes
///   trailing elementwise maps into (bias + activation after a convolution or matrix product).
/// - `map_out`: full loop vector ↦ the `Ro` write coordinates in `«So»`.
///   The reduction is folded away before the write, so it may only depend on the leading `Ro` indices.
/// - `maps#i`: full loop vector ↦ the `Ris#i` read coordinates of input `i`.
///   Transposes, slices and broadcasts are expressed here.
/// - `post_maps#j`: the `Ro` *output-cell* coordinates (the write coordinates `map_out` produces) ↦
///   the read coordinates of epilogue input `j`; epilogue inputs are read once per output cell, not
///   per loop iteration.
/// - `sched`: the op's **schedule**, selected in the frontend via the `Tileable` machinery below and
///   carried as an ordinary operand through fusion — a target-agnostic chooser (canonically a
///   `mk_sched` value) that, handed the target's loop-nest builder, returns the nest to run.
///   The lowering only binds it and **applies** the result to the op's decision-free fold step and
///   write-back, so unrolling, interchange and vectorization are plain IR, not lowering behavior.
///
/// The total loop count `Rn` is stated instead of the reduction count `Rr = Rn - Ro`: a
/// `core.nat.add (Ro, Rr)` in the domain cannot be inverted by type inference and every call site
/// would have to pass the whole meta group explicitly with `@`, whereas `Sr` pins `Rn` structurally.
///
/// `nis`, `nps`, `Ro`, `Rn`, `Ris` and `Rps` must be literals before the `tensor::Lower` phase; the
/// bounds `So` and `Sr` may stay symbolic.
///
axm map_reduce_post: {nis nps: Nat}
                   → {To Tp: *, Ro Rn: Nat, TSched: □}
                   → [So: «Ro; Nat», Sr: «Rn; Nat», sched: TSched]
                   → {Tis: «nis; *», Ris: «i: nis; Nat», Sis: «i: nis; «Ris#i; Nat»»,
                      Tps: «nps; *», Rps: «j: nps; Nat», Sps: «j: nps; «Rps#j; Nat»»}
                   → [f: Fn [To, «i: nis; Tis#i»] → To, init: To, post: Fn [To, «j: nps; Tps#j»] → Tp]
                   → [map_out: «Rn; affine.Index» → «Ro; affine.Index»]
                   → [     maps: «i: nis; «Rn; affine.Index» → «Ris#i; affine.Index»»,
                      post_maps: «j: nps; «Ro; affine.Index» → «Rps#j; affine.Index»»]
                   → [is: «i: nis; «Sis#i; Tis#i»», post_is: «j: nps; «Sps#j; Tps#j»»]
                   → «So; Tp»;
///
/// ### map_reduce_iter
///
/// Explicit iteration-domain variant: Ro is the output rank, Rp counts leading parallel iteration dims,
/// and Rn - Rp counts reductions. Legacy map_reduce_post keeps Rp = Ro and its original signature.
axm map_reduce_iter: {nis nps: Nat}
                   → {To Tp: *, Ro Rp Rn: Nat, TSched: □}
                   → [So: «Ro; Nat», Sr: «Rn; Nat», sched: TSched]
                   → {Tis: «nis; *», Ris: «i: nis; Nat», Sis: «i: nis; «Ris#i; Nat»»,
                      Tps: «nps; *», Rps: «j: nps; Nat», Sps: «j: nps; «Rps#j; Nat»»}
                   → [f: Fn [To, «i: nis; Tis#i»] → To, init: To, post: Fn [To, «j: nps; Tps#j»] → Tp]
                   → [map_out: «Rn; affine.Index» → «Ro; affine.Index»]
                   → [     maps: «i: nis; «Rn; affine.Index» → «Ris#i; affine.Index»»,
                      post_maps: «j: nps; «Ro; affine.Index» → «Rps#j; affine.Index»»]
                   → [is: «i: nis; «Sis#i; Tis#i»», post_is: «j: nps; «Sps#j; Tps#j»»]
                   → «So; Tp»;
///
/// ### SchedT
///
/// The type of a **schedule** value: a chooser over the target's loop-nest builder — for whatever nest
/// type `N` the lowering instantiates, it is handed the target's builder `mk` and returns the nest
/// to run.
let SchedT = [N: *] → ([vdim unroll tail pout rout ptail par: Nat] → N) → N;
///
/// ### mk_sched
///
/// The canonical schedule, picking
///
/// - `vdim`, the dim to run innermost as the unit-stride vector loop — a parallel dim runs over a row
///   of accumulators, the innermost **reduction** dim selects the reduction-vectorized nest (see
///   `btensor.mr_nest`), and `vdim ≥ Rn` means "none";
/// - `unroll`, the number of trailing reduction dims to unroll into straight-line code;
/// - `tail`, the loop partition of the innermost looped reduction dim: `0` for none, else its last iteration is peeled,
///   and the first unrolled dim runs only its first `tail` points in it (see `partition_red`) — the domain `Sr` then
///   overshoots, so a lowering that builds its own loops must skip those points (`btensor::tail_skip`);
/// - `pout`/`rout`, the loop order: the leading `rout` reduction dims run between the leading `pout` parallel dims and
///   the others (see `hoist_red`).
///
/// An alias for `btensor.mk_sched`, which `btensor` owns for the same reason it owns `btensor.Ring`.
anx mk_sched = btensor.mk_sched;
///
/// ### map_reduce
///
/// `map_reduce_post` without the epilogue and with the neutral schedule: delegates with `post = id`
/// (so `Tp = To`), no epilogue inputs (`nps = 0`), no vector dim (`vdim = Rn`, the out-of-range
/// sentinel), and no unrolling.
/// `Rn` is stated instead of `Rr` for the same reason as there.
anx lam map_reduce {nis: Nat} {To: *, Ro Rn: Nat}
                   (So: «Ro; Nat», Sr: «Rn; Nat»)
                   {Tis: «nis; *», Ris: «i: nis; Nat», Sis: «i: nis; «Ris#i; Nat»»}
                   (f: Fn [To, «i: nis; Tis#i»] → To, init: To)
                   (map_out: [«Rn; affine.Index»] → «Ro; affine.Index»)
                   (maps: «i: nis; ([«Rn; affine.Index»] → «Ris#i; affine.Index»)»)
                   (is: «i: nis; «Sis#i; Tis#i»»): «So; To» =
    map_reduce_post
        (So, Sr, mk_sched (Rn, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, Rn))
        (f, init, id @To) map_out (maps, ()) (is, ());
///
/// ### proj_map
///
/// `subs ↦ λ o. ‹j; o#(subs#j)›`: the access map that reads input axis `j` at loop variable `subs#j`,
/// e.g. `((0, 2), (2, 1))` for the two inputs of a matrix product.
/// An alias for `btensor.proj_map`, which `btensor` owns for the same reason it owns `btensor.Ring`.
anx proj_map = btensor.proj_map;
///
/// ## Shape Operations
///
/// Pure re-indexing: every element of the result is one element of an input, so the operation lives
/// entirely in the access map.
///
/// ### broadcast
///
/// Expands the dimensions of `input` to fit `s_out`:
/// for all `i`, either `s_in#i = s_out#i` or `s_in#i = 1`, and the size-1 dimensions are expanded to fit `s_out`.
///
/// Lowered directly to packs by the `LowerMapReduce` phase (`lower_broadcast`): a size-1 axis broadcast to `n`
/// becomes a `‹n; …›` pack rather than a materialized/looped copy, which `map_reduce` cannot express.
axm broadcast: {T: *, r: Nat}
             → [s_in s_out: «r; Nat», input: «s_in; T»]
             → «s_out; T», normalize_broadcast;
///
/// ### broadcast_in_dim
///
/// Transposes and expands the dimensions of `input` to fit `s_out`.
/// Each input dimension is mapped to the output dimension given by `index`; the holes are filled by broadcasting.
///
/// @todo We could probably implement this in terms of `broadcast` and `transpose` directly.
///
axm broadcast_in_dim: {T: *, r_in r_out: Nat}
                    → [s_in: «r_in; Nat», s_out: «r_out; Nat», input: «s_in; T», index: «r_in; Idx r_out»]
                    → «s_out; T», normalize_broadcast_in_dim;
///
/// ### concat_acc
///
/// Accumulator for the axis-sum: adds `row#ax` to the running total.
lam concat_acc {r: Nat} (ax: Idx r) (acc: Nat, row: «r; Nat»): Nat = acc + row#ax;
///
/// ### concat_shape
///
/// `(ax, Sis) ↦ s_out`: sums the inputs' extents along `ax`, keeps the shared extents on every other
/// axis.
lam concat_shape {nis r: Nat} (ax: Idx r, Sis: «nis; «r; Nat»»): «r; Nat» =
    let sum_ax = vec.fold.l (concat_acc ax) (0, Sis);
    let row_0  = Sis#(core.idx 0 nis 0);
    ‹d: r; core.select (core.icmp.e (d, ax), sum_ax, row_0#d)›;
///
/// ### concat
///
/// Joins `nis` inputs along axis `ax`. All inputs share rank `r` and agree on every axis except `ax`;
/// the output extent along `ax` is the sum of the inputs' extents there (`s_out#ax = Σ_i Sis#i#ax`).
/// `out[…o…] = is#k[… o with o#ax ↦ o#ax − off#k …]`, where `k` is the input whose range `o#ax` lands
/// in (`off` are the prefix sums of the per-input extents along `ax`).
///
/// The output shape is deduced from `ax` and the per-input shapes `Sis` (see `concat_shape`), so it is
/// not passed explicitly.

axm concat: {T: *, nis r: Nat}
          → [ax: Idx r]
          → {Sis: «i: nis; «r; Nat»»}
          → [is: «i: nis; «Sis#i; T»»]
          → «concat_shape (ax, Sis); T», normalize_concat;
///
/// ### flip
///
/// Reverses every axis: `out[…d…] = input[… (s_in#d − 1) − d …]`.
///
axm flip: {T: *, r: Nat}
        → [s_in: «r; Nat»]
        → [input: «s_in; T»]
        → «s_in; T», normalize_flip;
///
/// ### pad_shape
///
lam pad_shape {r: Nat} (lo s_in hi: «r; Nat»): «r; Nat» =
    ‹d: r; lo#d + s_in#d + hi#d›;
///
/// ### pad
///
/// Pads each axis: `s_out#d = lo#d + s_in#d + hi#d`, rank preserved.
/// `out[…d…] = input[… o#d − lo#d …]` whenever every axis lands inside the input region;
/// otherwise the value depends on `mode`:
///
/// - `mode = 0` (constant): out-of-region cells take the scalar `value`.
/// - `mode = 1` (replicate): out-of-region cells take the nearest edge element (the read index is
///   clamped to `[0, s_in#d − 1]` per axis); `value` is ignored.
///
/// The output shape is deduced from `lo`, `s_in` and `hi` (see `pad_shape`), so it is not passed explicitly.

// `(lo, s_in, hi) ↦ ‹d; lo#d + s_in#d + hi#d›`: the padded shape.

axm pad: {T: *, r: Nat}
       → [s_in: «r; Nat»]
       → [mode: Nat, lo hi: «r; Nat»]
       → [input: «s_in; T», value: T]
       → «pad_shape (lo, s_in, hi); T», normalize_pad;
///
/// ### repeat
///
/// Tiles `input` to the larger shape `s_out` (each `s_out#d` must be a multiple of `s_in#d`): `out[…d…] = input[… d mod s_in#d …]`.
///
axm repeat: {T: *, r: Nat}
          → [s_in: «r; Nat»]
          → [s_out: «r; Nat»]
          → [input: «s_in; T»]
          → «s_out; T», normalize_repeat;
///
/// ### reshape
///
/// Row-major reshape: `prod(s_in)` must equal `prod(s_out)`. Loops over the result and reads the input at the
/// delinearized linear index, so it works for arbitrary rank changes (e.g. flatten/unflatten).
///
/// `s_in` is explicit because `s_out` has a different rank and so cannot pin the input's
/// rank/element-type during inference.
axm reshape: {T: *, r_in r_out: Nat}
           → [s_in: «r_in; Nat»]
           → [s_out: «r_out; Nat»]
           → [input: «s_in; T»]
           → «s_out; T», normalize_reshape;
///
/// ### slice
///
/// Strided slice / narrow: `out[…d…] = input[… start#d + step#d · d …]`. Same rank in/out; `s_out` gives the sliced
/// extents (the caller must ensure `start#d + step#d · (s_out#d − 1) < s_in#d`).
///
axm slice: {T: *, r: Nat}
         → [s_in: «r; Nat»]
         → [start step s_out: «r; Nat»]
         → [input: «s_in; T»]
         → «s_out; T», normalize_slice;
///
/// ### transpose_shape
///
/// `(s, permutation) ↦ ‹i; s#(permutation⁻¹#i)›`: the permuted shape.
lam transpose_shape {r: Nat} (s: «r; Nat», permutation: «r; Idx r»): «r; Nat» =
  let shape_permutation = ‹i: r; option.unwrap_unsafe (vec.first core.icmp.e (permutation, i))›;
  ‹i: r; s#(shape_permutation#i)›;
///
/// ### transpose
///
/// Permutes the dimensions of `input` according to `permutation`.
///
/// `permutation` precedes `input`, and `{s}` is a *separate* implicit group introduced after it, so that `{T, r, s}`
/// can be inferred: `permutation: «r; Idx r»` pins the rank `r`, and only then is the `{s}` Hole created — with the
/// now-literal type `«r; Nat»`, which lets `input: «s; T»` read off exactly the first `r` axes of its shape.
/// Merging the two groups would not do: the domain `«s; T»` is built once, while `r` is still unknown.
axm transpose: {T: *, r: Nat}
             → [permutation: «r; Idx r»]
             → {s: «r; Nat»}
             → [input: «s; T»]
             → «transpose_shape (s, permutation); T», normalize_transpose;
///
/// ### transpose_2d
///
/// Permutes the dimensions of a 2-dimensional tensor.
///
anx lam transpose_2d {T: *} {s: «2; Nat»} (input: «s; T»): «s#tt, s#ff; T» = transpose (tt, ff) input;
///
/// ## Element-wise Operations
///
/// Apply a scalar function at every coordinate.
///
/// ### binary
///
/// Maps a binary function over a pair of tensors.
///
axm binary: {Ti1 Ti2 To: *}
          → [app: [Ti1, Ti2] → To]
          → {r: Nat, s: «r; Nat»}
          → [is: [«s; Ti1», «s; Ti2»]]
          → «s; To»;
///
/// ### map
///
/// Maps a function over a collection of tensors.
///
axm map: {T: *, ni: Nat, Is: «ni; *»}
       → [app: «i: ni; Is#i» → T]
       → {r: Nat, s: «r; Nat»}
       → [is: «i: ni; «s; Is#i»»]
       → «s; T»;
///
/// ### select
///
/// Maps `core.select` over tensors.
///
axm select: {T: *}
          → {r: Nat, s: «r; Nat»}
          → [is: [«s; Bool», «s; T», «s; T»]]
          → «s; T»;
///
/// ### unary
///
/// Maps a unary function over a tensor.
///
axm unary: {Ti To: *}
         → [app: Ti → To]
         → {r: Nat, s: «r; Nat»}
         → [i: «s; Ti»]
         → «s; To»;
///
/// ## Reductions
///
/// Operations that fold one or more reduction dims away.
///
/// ### bmm
///
/// Computes the batch matrix multiplication (BMM) of two 3-dimensional tensors — a special case of `dot_product`.
///
axm bmm: [R: Ring]
       → {B M K N: Nat}
       → [t1: «B, M, K; R#T», t2: «B, K, N; R#T»]
       → «B, M, N; R#T»;
///
/// ### conv_shape
///
/// `(n, cout, (H, W), (KH, KW), stride, dilation, padding) ↦ (n, cout, OH, OW)`, where
/// \f$O_d = (s_d + 2 \cdot \mathit{padding}_d - (\mathit{dilation}_d \cdot (k_d - 1) + 1)) / \mathit{stride}_d + 1\f$:
/// the NCHW output shape.
pub lam conv_shape (n cout: Nat, s k stride dilation padding: «2; Nat»): «4; Nat» =
    let (hwx, hwy) = ‹d: 2; (s#(d) + 2 * padding#(d) - (dilation#(d) * (k#(d) - 1) + 1)) / stride#(d) + 1›;
    (n, cout, hwx, hwy);
///
/// ### conv
///
/// 2-D convolution (cross-correlation) in the ring `R`, `groups = 1`, no bias:
/// \f[
///     \mathit{out}_{n,co,oh,ow} = \sum_{ci,kh,kw} \mathit{padded}_{n,ci,ih,iw} \cdot \mathit{weight}_{co,ci,kh,kw},
///     \qquad ih = \mathit{stride}_0 \cdot oh + \mathit{dilation}_0 \cdot kh,
///     \qquad iw = \mathit{stride}_1 \cdot ow + \mathit{dilation}_1 \cdot kw
/// \f]
///
/// - Layout: `input: «N, Cin, H, W; R#T»`, `weight: «Cout, Cin, KH, KW; R#T»`,
///   `output: «N, Cout, OH, OW; R#T»`.
/// - `padding` zero-pads the spatial axes (composed via `pad`), so the windowed read is a pure affine
///   map.
/// - The output shape follows from the extents and window parameters (see `conv_shape`) and is not
///   passed explicitly.

axm conv: [R: Ring]
        → {n cin cout h w kh kw: Nat}
        → [stride dilation padding: «2; Nat»]
        → [input: «n, cin, h, w; R#T», weight: «cout, cin, kh, kw; R#T»]
        → «conv_shape (n, cout, (h, w), (kh, kw), stride, dilation, padding); R#T»;
///
/// ### dot_general_shape
///
/// The result shape: the batching extents, then the left operand's free extents, then the right
/// operand's; the batching and contracting extents are checked to agree.
lam dot_general_shape {r1 r2: Nat} {nc nb: Nat}
  (c1: «nc; Idx r1», c2: «nc; Idx r2», b1: «nb; Idx r1», b2: «nb; Idx r2»)
  (s1: «r1; Nat», s2: «r2; Nat») =
      let bs_check = ‹i: nb; refly.check (s1#(b1#i) == s2#(b2#i), s1#(b1#i), "batching dims don't match")›;
      let cs_check = ‹i: nc; refly.check (s1#(c1#i) == s2#(c2#i), s1#(c1#i), "contracting dims don't match")›;
      let bs       = ‹i: nb; s1#(b1#i)›;
      let bc_1     = vec.cat (b1, c1);
      let s1_res   = vec.diff (s1, bc_1);
      let bc_2     = vec.cat (b2, c2);
      let s2_res   = vec.diff (s2, bc_2);
      let s12_res  = vec.cat (s1_res, s2_res);
      let s_out    = vec.cat (bs, s12_res);
      s_out;
///
/// ### dot_product
///
/// Returns the [generalized dot product](https://openxla.org/stablehlo/spec#dot_general) of `a` and `b`:
///
/// - `R`: the ring in which the dot product is performed
/// - `r1`/`r2`: the ranks of the two inputs
/// - `nc`/`nb`: the number of contracting/batching dimensions
/// - `c1`/`c2`: the contracting dimensions of the left/right input
/// - `b1`/`b2`: the batching dimensions of the left/right input
/// - `s1`/`s2`: the shape of the left/right input
/// - `a`/`b`: the left/right input
///
axm dot_product: [R: Ring]
               → {r1 r2: Nat}
               → {nc nb: Nat}
               → [c1: «nc; Idx r1», c2: «nc; Idx r2», b1: «nb; Idx r1», b2: «nb; Idx r2»]
               → {s1: «r1; Nat», s2: «r2; Nat»}
               → [a: «s1; R#T», b: «s2; R#T»]
               → «dot_general_shape (c1, c2, b1, b2) (s1, s2); R#T»;
///
/// ### pool
///
/// 2-D pooling: reduces each `(kernel#0, kernel#1)` window of `input: «N, C, H, W; T»` with the fold
/// `g` seeded by `init` to `output: «N, C, OH, OW; T»`, each channel independently.
///
/// - `padding` pads the spatial axes with `init` (composed via `pad`), so padded cells are inert.
/// - Max-pooling is `g = max, init = −∞`; sum-/average-pooling is `g = +, init = 0` — divide by the
///   window size afterwards for the average.
/// - Unlike `conv` there is no weight, so the window size `kernel` is an explicit operand, and the
///   output spatial size `s_out = (OH, OW)` is passed explicitly (no `Nat` division to compute it).

axm pool: {T: *}
        → [g: [T, T] → T, init: T]
        → {n c h w: Nat}
        → [kernel stride dilation padding s_out: «2; Nat»]
        → [input: «n, c, h, w; T»]
        → «n, c, s_out#0₂, s_out#1₂; T»;
///
/// ### product_2d
///
/// Computes the matrix product of two 2-dimensional tensors — a special case of `dot_product`.
///
axm product_2d: [R: Ring]
              → {m k l: Nat}
              → [t1: «m, k; R#T», t2: «k, l; R#T»]
              → «m, l; R#T»;
///
/// ## Indexing
///
/// Data-dependent access: an `index` tensor picks the coordinate along `dim`.
///
/// ### gather
///
/// Reads one source element for every index element:
///
/// - the output coordinate is also the index coordinate, except on `dim`, where the value loaded from
///   `index` selects the source coordinate;
/// - `s_idx#d <= s_src#d` is required for every `d != dim`;
/// - `dim: Idx r` and the index element type encode the axis and the indexed-source bounds in the type.
///
/// ```python
/// for out_index in ndindex(s_idx):
///     selected = index[out_index]
///     source_index = tuple(
///         selected if d == dim else out_index[d]
///         for d in range(r)
///     )
///     output[out_index] = input[source_index]
/// ```
axm gather: {T: *, r: Nat}
          → [s_src: «r; Nat», s_idx: «r; Nat»]
          → [dim: Idx r]
          → [input: «s_src; T», index: «s_idx; Idx (s_src#dim)»]
          → «s_idx; T»;
///
/// ### scatter
///
/// Starts with `input` and visits `index` in row-major order.
/// Each corresponding `updates` element is stored at the input coordinate selected by `index` on `dim`.
/// Duplicate destinations use the last visited update. For `d != dim`, `s_idx#d <= s_src#d`; on every axis, `s_idx#d <= s_updates#d`.
///
/// ```python
/// output = input.copy()
/// for update_coord in ndindex(s_idx):
///     selected = index[update_coord]
///     destination = tuple(
///         selected if d == dim else update_coord[d]
///         for d in range(r)
///     )
///     output[destination] = updates[update_coord]
/// ```
axm scatter: {T: *, r: Nat}
           → [s_src: «r; Nat», s_idx: «r; Nat», s_updates: «r; Nat»]
           → [dim: Idx r]
           → [input: «s_src; T», index: «s_idx; Idx (s_src#dim)», updates: «s_updates; T»]
           → «s_src; T»;
///
/// ## Reflection
///
/// Compile-time queries about an operand that a schedule selector (see Tiling) branches on.
///
/// ### fastest_axis
///
/// Layout reflection for schedule selectors: which axis of the rank-`r` tensor `t` is the
/// fastest-varying (unit-stride) axis of the tensor **actually** read, once `fuse_tensor`'s
/// read-through has absorbed a pure re-indexed read behind `t` — behind a transpose the source's
/// trailing axis maps back to a different axis of `t`.
///
/// - a plain tensor (anything that is not such a read, a still-symbolic operand included): its own
///   last axis `r − 1`.
/// - an undecidable read (composed arithmetic like `reshape`, a nested read chain): the sentinel `r`.
///
/// This reflects only operand structure the frontend cannot inspect itself; the schedule **decision**
/// built on the answer stays in the IR next to the op (see `dot_product_impl`).
/// `tensor::Lower` applies it where the op's operands are concrete.
///
axm fastest_axis: {T: *} → [r: Nat, t: T] → Nat, normalize_fastest_axis;
///
/// ### if_static
///
/// Binding-time dispatch for schedule selectors, so that one selector serves compile-time and
/// runtime extents without staging annotations at the call site:
///
/// - `k` a literal: the normalizer picks `s`.
/// - `k` symbolic: stays stuck, keeping the choice open until instantiation.
/// - still stuck at lowering time: the lowerings residualize to `d`, since an undecided `k` is then a
///   runtime value.
axm if_static: {T: *} → [k: Nat, s d: T] → T, normalize_if_static;
///
/// ### target
///
/// The target's register and cache budget, which the schedule selectors derive their blocking from:
///
/// - `vec_bytes`, the width of a vector register, and `vec_regs`, how many there are;
/// - `acc_regs`, how many of them a register block may fill with accumulators — about half on a load/store ISA, whose
///   operands need registers of their own, more where an FMA takes a memory operand;
/// - `cache_elems`, the elements of an operand panel that stay in cache across the loops reusing it.
///
/// Each defaults to the host; `-X tensor:<name>=<n>` overrides it, so one selector serves every target.
axm target.(vec_bytes, vec_regs, acc_regs, cache_elems, threads): [] → Nat, normalize_target;
///
/// ## Tiling
///
/// Tiling, strip-mining and schedule selection are implemented entirely in the frontend IR, with
/// partial evaluation doing the rewriting; no C++ transformation code.
///
/// ### Tileable
///
/// A `Tileable` existentially packages a computation over an affinely-indexed iteration domain
/// together with its **schedule** (cf. MLIR's TilingInterface):
///
/// - `Tp`: the element type of the result «So; Tp», whose rank `Ro` is independent of the iteration rank.
/// - the iteration domain `Sr` (leading `Rp` parallel dims, trailing `Rr` reduction dims) and the
///   result shape `So` — MLIR's getIterationDomain + getLoopIteratorTypes.
/// - `oax`: which output axis each parallel dim writes — the piece of the write map the domain
///   transforms need (`strip_mine_par` splits the output along `oax#d`); `Ro` marks a fused iteration dim that
///   no longer corresponds to a single output axis.
/// - the schedule `vdim`/`unroll`/`tail`/`pout`/`rout`/`ptail`/`par` (see `mk_sched`): the chooser `retile` packs into the op.
/// - `retile`: rebuild the computation over a *reindexed* domain, given the new domain and
///   schedule, `iota` (maps new loop vectors to old loop vectors) and `omega` (maps (old output
///   coordinates, new loop vector) to new output coordinates).
///
/// The op's internals (combiner, init, epilogue, inputs, access maps) stay hidden in the closure.
let Tileable = [
    Tp: *,
    Ro: Nat, Rp: Nat, Rr: Nat,
    So: «Ro; Nat»,
    oax: «Rp; Nat»,
    Sr: «Rp + Rr; Nat»,
    vdim: Nat, unroll: Nat, tail: Nat, pout: Nat, rout: Nat, ptail: Nat, par: Nat,
    retile: [Ro_n Rp_n Rr_n: Nat]
          → [So_n: «Ro_n; Nat», Sr_n: «Rp_n + Rr_n; Nat», vdim_n unroll_n tail_n pout_n rout_n ptail_n par_n: Nat]
          → [iota:  «Rp_n + Rr_n; affine.Index» → «Rp + Rr; affine.Index»,
             omega: [«Ro; affine.Index», «Rp_n + Rr_n; affine.Index»] → «Ro_n; affine.Index»]
          → «So_n; Tp»,
];
///
/// #### comp_map
///
/// Composition helper for access maps: (m ∘ g).
///
lam comp_map {A B C: *} (m: B → C, g: A → B) (o: A): C = m (g o);
///
/// #### interchange
///
/// Permute the loop dims — new loop `j` iterates old dim `perm#j`. Loop order and write
/// position are decoupled by the write map, so So is untouched. The permutation must keep parallel
/// dims parallel (`perm#j < Rp` iff `j < Rp`), enforced at compile time; the schedule's `vdim` moves to
/// its new position.
anx lam interchange (t: Tileable, perm: «t#Rp + t#Rr; Nat»): Tileable =
    let r = t#Rp + t#Rr;
    let perm_ok = ‹j: r; let jj = core.bitcast Nat j;
                         refly.check (core.icmp.e @2 (jj < t#Rp, perm#j < t#Rp),
                                       perm#j,
                                       "interchange: permutation must not mix parallel and reduction dims")›;
    let sr_n   = ‹i: r; t#Sr#(core.idx 0 r (perm_ok#i))›;
    let vdim_n = match vec.first `== (perm_ok, t#vdim) with
                     | j: Idx r => core.bitcast Nat j
                     | _: []    => t#vdim;
    let par_n  = match vec.first `== (perm_ok, t#par) with
                     | j: Idx r => core.bitcast Nat j
                     | _: []    => t#par;
    // The parallel dims permute among themselves (checked above), so the new dim j writes the
    // output axis its old dim perm#j wrote.
    let oax_n = ‹j: t#Rp; t#oax#(core.idx 0 (t#Rp) (perm_ok#(core.idx 0 r (core.bitcast Nat j))))›;
    // iota: old dim i is found at the new position j with perm#j = i.
    lam iota_p (o: «r; affine.Index»): «r; affine.Index» =
        ‹i: r; o#(option.unwrap_unsafe (vec.first `== (perm_ok, core.bitcast Nat i)))›;
    lam retile_n (ro_m rp_m rr_m: Nat)
                 (so_m: «ro_m; Nat», sr_m: «rp_m + rr_m; Nat», vdim_m unroll_m tail_m pout_m rout_m ptail_m par_m: Nat)
                 (iota:  «rp_m + rr_m; affine.Index» → «r; affine.Index»,
                  omega: [«t#Ro; affine.Index», «rp_m + rr_m; affine.Index»] → «ro_m; affine.Index»)
               : «so_m; t#Tp» =
        t#retile (ro_m, rp_m, rr_m) (so_m, sr_m, vdim_m, unroll_m, tail_m, pout_m, rout_m, ptail_m, par_m)
                 (comp_map (iota_p, iota), omega);
    (t#Tp, t#Ro, t#Rp, t#Rr, t#So, oax_n, sr_n, vdim_n, t#unroll, t#tail, t#pout, t#rout, t#ptail, par_n, retile_n);
///
/// #### fuse_iter
///
/// Fuse two adjacent iteration dims in row-major order, reducing the iteration rank by one and preserving So.
/// Both dims must be parallel or both reduction dims; fusion preserves sequential reduction order. Apply fusion
/// before vectorization or across dims outside its vector/partition windows. A hoisted reduction boundary or the
/// trailing unroll boundary may not separate the pair. Loop annotations are reindexed; output axes are unchanged.
anx lam fuse_iter (t: Tileable, d: Nat): Tileable =
    let r = t#Rp + t#Rr;
    let d = refly.check (d + 1 < r, d, "fuse_iter: expected two adjacent iteration dims");
    let d = refly.check (core.icmp.e @2 (d < t#Rp, d + 1 < t#Rp), d,
                         "fuse_iter: cannot mix parallel and reduction dims");
    let d = refly.check (core.bit2.and_ 2 (t#vdim != d, t#vdim != d + 1), d,
                         "fuse_iter: cannot fuse the vector dim");
    let is_par = d < t#Rp;
    let d = refly.check (core.bit2.or_ 2 (t#rout == 0,
                         (t#Rp + t#rout != d + 1, t#pout != d + 1)#is_par), d,
                         "fuse_iter: cannot cross a hoisted reduction");
    let d = refly.check (core.bit2.or_ 2 (t#ptail == 0,
                         core.bit2.and_ 2 (t#vdim != d + 1, t#vdim != d + 2)), d,
                         "fuse_iter: cannot fuse a partitioned parallel dim");
    let u_lo = r - t#unroll;
    let d = refly.check (core.bit2.or_ 2 (is_par, u_lo != d + 1), d,
                         "fuse_iter: cannot cross the trailing unroll boundary");
    let d = refly.check (core.bit2.or_ 2 (t#tail == 0,
                         core.bit2.or_ 2 (is_par, core.bit2.or_ 2 (d + 2 < u_lo, u_lo < d))), d,
                         "fuse_iter: cannot fuse a partitioned reduction dim");
    let rp_n = (t#Rp, t#Rp - 1)#is_par;
    let rr_n = (t#Rr - 1, t#Rr)#is_par;
    let r_n = rp_n + rr_n;
    let a = t#Sr#(core.idx 0 r d);
    let b = t#Sr#(core.idx 0 r (d + 1));
    let sr_n = ‹j: r_n; let jj = core.bitcast Nat j;
                        core.select (jj < d, t#Sr#(core.idx 0 r jj),
                        core.select (jj == d, a * b, t#Sr#(core.idx 0 r (jj + 1))))›;
    let oax_n = ‹j: rp_n; let jj = core.bitcast Nat j;
                          core.select (core.bit2.and_ 2 (is_par, jj == d), t#Ro,
                          t#oax#(core.idx 0 (t#Rp) (jj + (0, 1)#(core.bit2.and_ 2 (is_par, d < jj)))))›;
    // Empty domains execute no iterations, but their maps must still elaborate without division by zero.
    let divisor = (b, 1)#(b == 0);
    lam iota_f (o: «r_n; affine.Index»): «r; affine.Index» =
        ‹j: r; let jj = core.bitcast Nat j;
               core.select (jj < d, o#(core.idx 0 r_n jj),
               core.select (jj == d, affine.semiop.floordiv (o#(core.idx 0 r_n d), divisor),
               core.select (jj == d + 1, affine.semiop.rem (o#(core.idx 0 r_n d), divisor),
               o#(core.idx 0 r_n (jj - 1)))))›;
    lam retile_n (ro_m rp_m rr_m: Nat)
                 (so_m: «ro_m; Nat», sr_m: «rp_m + rr_m; Nat», vdim_m unroll_m tail_m pout_m rout_m ptail_m par_m: Nat)
                 (iota: «rp_m + rr_m; affine.Index» → «r_n; affine.Index»,
                  omega: [«t#Ro; affine.Index», «rp_m + rr_m; affine.Index»] → «ro_m; affine.Index»)
               : «so_m; t#Tp» =
        t#retile (ro_m, rp_m, rr_m) (so_m, sr_m, vdim_m, unroll_m, tail_m, pout_m, rout_m, ptail_m, par_m)
                 (comp_map (iota_f, iota), omega);
    let vdim_n = t#vdim - (0, 1)#(d < t#vdim);
    let par_n = t#par - (0, 1)#(d < t#par);
    let unroll_n = t#unroll - (0, 1)#(core.bit2.and_ 2 ((tt, ff)#is_par, u_lo <= d));
    let pout_n = t#pout - (0, 1)#(core.bit2.and_ 2 (is_par, d < t#pout));
    let rout_n = t#rout - (0, 1)#(core.bit2.and_ 2 ((tt, ff)#is_par, d < t#Rp + t#rout));
    (t#Tp, t#Ro, rp_n, rr_n, t#So, oax_n, sr_n, vdim_n, unroll_n, t#tail, pout_n, rout_n, t#ptail, par_n, retile_n);
///
/// #### fuse_par
///
/// Compatibility entry point restricted to parallel iteration dims. Like fuse_iter, it preserves the output layout.
anx lam fuse_par (t: Tileable, d: Nat): Tileable =
    fuse_iter (t, refly.check (d + 1 < t#Rp, d, "fuse_par: expected two adjacent parallel dims"));
///
/// #### materialize
///
/// Materialize a `Tileable` over its current domain and schedule (identity reindexing).
anx lam materialize (t: Tileable): «t#So; t#Tp» =
    lam id_i (o: «t#Rp + t#Rr; affine.Index»): «t#Rp + t#Rr; affine.Index» = o;
    lam id_o (oo: «t#Ro; affine.Index», o: «t#Rp + t#Rr; affine.Index»): «t#Ro; affine.Index» = oo;
    t#retile (t#Ro, t#Rp, t#Rr) (t#So, t#Sr, t#vdim, t#unroll, t#tail, t#pout, t#rout, t#ptail, t#par) (id_i, id_o);
///
/// #### mr_iter_tileable
///
/// The map-reduce instance with independent output and iteration ranks, the SSA analog of MLIR's getTiledImplementation: `retile` rebuilds
/// the same op over the new domain, composing the access maps with `iota` and the write map with
/// `omega`.
///
/// - No extract/insert_slice machinery — the tiled reads hit the original inputs through the
///   reindexed access maps.
/// - Epilogue-free (`nps = 0`): impls build raw ops and the epilogue arrives later via `fuse_tensor`,
///   because epilogue-input access maps take output-cell coordinates, whose rank a domain transform
///   may change.
/// - `oax` states which output axis `map_out` writes each parallel dim to — the identity for the
///   impls' projection write maps; use the sentinel Ro for a reindexed dim.
anx lam mr_iter_tileable
                {nis: Nat}
                {Ro Rp Rr: Nat}
                {To Tp: *}
                (So: «Ro; Nat», oax: «Rp; Nat», Sr: «Rp + Rr; Nat»)
                {Tis: «nis; *», Ris: «i: nis; Nat», Sis: «i: nis; «Ris#i; Nat»»}
                (f: Fn [To, «i: nis; Tis#i»] → To, init: To, post: Fn [To, []] → Tp)
                (map_out: [«Rp + Rr; affine.Index»] → «Ro; affine.Index»)
                (maps: «i: nis; ([«Rp + Rr; affine.Index»] → «Ris#i; affine.Index»)»)
                (is: «i: nis; «Sis#i; Tis#i»»)
              : Tileable =
    lam retile (Ro_n Rp_n Rr_n: Nat)
               (So_n: «Ro_n; Nat», Sr_n: «Rp_n + Rr_n; Nat», vdim_n unroll_n tail_n pout_n rout_n ptail_n par_n: Nat)
               (iota:  «Rp_n + Rr_n; affine.Index» → «Rp + Rr; affine.Index»,
                omega: [«Ro; affine.Index», «Rp_n + Rr_n; affine.Index»] → «Ro_n; affine.Index»)
             : «So_n; Tp» =
        lam map_out_n (o: «Rp_n + Rr_n; affine.Index»): «Ro_n; affine.Index» =
            omega (map_out (iota o), o);
        let sched = mk_sched (vdim_n, unroll_n, tail_n, pout_n, rout_n, ptail_n, par_n);
        let maps_n = ‹i: nis; comp_map (maps#i, iota)›;
        lam legacy (u: []): «So_n; Tp» =
            map_reduce_post @(nis, 0) @(To, Tp, Ro_n, Rp_n + Rr_n, SchedT)
                (So_n, Sr_n, sched) @(Tis, Ris, Sis, (), (), ()) (f, init, post)
                map_out_n (maps_n, ()) (is, ());
        lam explicit (u: []): «So_n; Tp» =
            map_reduce_iter @(nis, 0) @(To, Tp, Ro_n, Rp_n, Rp_n + Rr_n, SchedT)
                (So_n, Sr_n, sched) @(Tis, Ris, Sis, (), (), ()) (f, init, post)
                map_out_n (maps_n, ()) (is, ());
        (explicit, legacy)#(Ro_n == Rp_n) ();
    (Tp, Ro, Rp, Rr, So, oax, Sr, Rp + Rr, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, Rp + Rr, retile);
///
/// #### mr_tileable
///
/// Compatibility constructor for a canonical domain: one parallel dim per output axis (Rp = Ro).
anx lam mr_tileable
                {nis: Nat}
                {Ro Rr: Nat}
                {To Tp: *}
                (So: «Ro; Nat», oax: «Ro; Nat», Sr: «Ro + Rr; Nat»)
                {Tis: «nis; *», Ris: «i: nis; Nat», Sis: «i: nis; «Ris#i; Nat»»}
                (f: Fn [To, «i: nis; Tis#i»] → To, init: To, post: Fn [To, []] → Tp)
                (map_out: [«Ro + Rr; affine.Index»] → «Ro; affine.Index»)
                (maps: «i: nis; ([«Ro + Rr; affine.Index»] → «Ris#i; affine.Index»)»)
                (is: «i: nis; «Sis#i; Tis#i»»)
              : Tileable =
    mr_iter_tileable @nis @(Ro, Ro, Rr) @(To, Tp) (So, oax, Sr) @(Tis, Ris, Sis) (f, init, post) map_out maps is;
///
/// #### unroll_trailing
///
/// Fully unroll the trailing `d` reduction dims into the loop body — e.g. a
/// convolution's kh·kw window (`d = 2`). Their trip counts must be literals at lowering time.
anx lam unroll_trailing (t: Tileable, d: Nat): Tileable =
    (t#Tp, t#Ro, t#Rp, t#Rr, t#So, t#oax, t#Sr, t#vdim, d, t#tail, t#pout, t#rout, t#ptail, t#par, t#retile);
///
/// #### vectorize
///
/// Run dim `d` as the innermost, unit-stride vector loop (per-cell fold order is
/// unchanged). A parallel `d` runs inside the reduction loops over a row of accumulators; the
/// innermost reduction dim as `d` selects `btensor.mr_nest`'s reduction-vectorized nest instead.
anx lam vectorize (t: Tileable, d: Nat): Tileable =
    (t#Tp, t#Ro, t#Rp, t#Rr, t#So, t#oax, t#Sr, d, t#unroll, t#tail, t#pout, t#rout, t#ptail, t#par, t#retile);
///
/// #### hoist_red
///
/// Run the leading `rout` reduction dims between the leading `pout` parallel dims and the others (TVM's
/// `decompose_reduction` plus a `reorder` across the reduction): the inner parallel dims' accumulators then stay
/// live across the hoisted loops, the reduction-outer order of a cache-blocked contraction.
/// Only the tiled nest of `btensor.mr_nest` honors it, which needs a parallel vector dim and no `tail`; the loop
/// order does not change the result.
lam hoist_red (t: Tileable, pout rout: Nat): Tileable =
    (t#Tp, t#Ro, t#Rp, t#Rr, t#So, t#oax, t#Sr, t#vdim, t#unroll, t#tail, pout, rout, t#ptail, t#par, t#retile);
///
/// #### parallelize
///
/// Run parallel dim `d` on the runtime's threads (see `btensor.par_for`); it must be the outermost loop.
anx lam parallelize (t: Tileable, d: Nat): Tileable =
    (t#Tp, t#Ro, t#Rp, t#Rr, t#So, t#oax, t#Sr, t#vdim, t#unroll, t#tail, t#pout, t#rout, t#ptail, d, t#retile);
///
/// ### Strip-Mining
///
/// Strip-mining is a pure **domain** transform (cf. TilingInterface's `op[N] → op[N/ts, ts]`) that
/// `retile` absorbs entirely: `iota` recombines the split loop vector, and for a parallel dim `omega`
/// splits the write coordinate.
/// The result is an ordinary `map_reduce_post` over a higher-rank domain with composed access maps —
/// schedule operand and lowering untouched.
/// Classic cache tiling is composition: strip-mine, then `interchange` the tile loops outward.
///
/// #### check_div
///
/// Checks that `ts` exactly divides the extent `sd` (discharges symbolically for factored extents).
lam check_div (sd ts: Nat): Nat =
    refly.check (sd / ts * ts == sd, sd, "strip_mine: tile size must divide the extent");
///
/// #### shift_ax
///
/// Axis renumbering after inserting a new axis at position `a`.
lam shift_ax (a x: Nat): Nat = core.select (x < a, x, x + 1);
///
/// #### split_bounds
///
/// Split bounds vector `v` (length `n`) at dim `d` into `(nt, ts)`; the result arity `m` (`= n+1`) is
/// passed explicitly, in the syntactic form the call site's types require (the checker does not
/// re-associate `core.nat.add` symbolically).
lam split_bounds (m n: Nat, v: «n; Nat», d ts nt: Nat): «m; Nat» =
    ‹i: m; let ii = core.bitcast Nat i;
           core.select (ii < d, v#(core.idx 0 n ii),
           core.select (ii == d, nt,
           core.select (ii == d + 1, ts,
           v#(core.idx 0 n (ii - 1)))))›;
///
/// #### split_iota
///
/// Recombine a split loop vector (`m = n+1`): `old#d = new#d · ts + new#(d+1)`.
lam split_iota (m n d ts: Nat) (o: «m; affine.Index»): «n; affine.Index» =
    ‹j: n; let jj = core.bitcast Nat j;
           core.select (jj < d, o#(core.idx 0 m jj),
           core.select (jj == d,
                         affine.op.add (affine.semiop.mul (o#(core.idx 0 m d), ts),
                                         o#(core.idx 0 m (d + 1))),
           o#(core.idx 0 m (jj + 1))))›;
///
/// #### split_omega
///
/// Split an output coordinate vector (`m = n+1`): `new#a = old#a ⌊/⌋ ts`, `new#(a+1) = old#a mod ts`.
lam split_omega (m n a ts: Nat) (oc: «n; affine.Index»): «m; affine.Index» =
    ‹j: m; let jj = core.bitcast Nat j;
           core.select (jj < a, oc#(core.idx 0 n jj),
           core.select (jj == a, affine.semiop.floordiv (oc#(core.idx 0 n a), ts),
           core.select (jj == a + 1, affine.semiop.rem (oc#(core.idx 0 n a), ts),
           oc#(core.idx 0 n (jj - 1)))))›;
///
/// #### split_par
///
/// Split parallel dim `d` (d < Rp) into two adjacent parallel dims (nt, ts) with the schedule's `ptail`. The result
/// shape splits along the output axis `d` writes (`oax#d`), so the materialized tensor is *packed*
/// (`«…, nt, ts, …»` along that axis).
lam split_par (t: Tileable, d ts nt ptail: Nat): Tileable =
    let r    = t#Rp + t#Rr;
    let ro_n = t#Ro + 1;
    let rp_n = t#Rp + 1;
    let r_n  = t#Rp + 1 + t#Rr;
    let a    = t#oax#(core.idx 0 (t#Rp) d);
    let par_n = core.select (t#par < t#Rp, core.select (t#par <= d, t#par, t#par + 1), r_n);
    // The split dim writes the split axis pair; all other dims write their old axis, renumbered
    // around the insertion at `a`. The unit-stride half is the inner one: a vdim at `d` moves to
    // `d + 1`.
    let oax_n = ‹j: rp_n; let jj = core.bitcast Nat j;
                          core.select (jj < d, shift_ax (a, t#oax#(core.idx 0 (t#Rp) jj)),
                          core.select (jj == d, a,
                          core.select (jj == d + 1, a + 1,
                          shift_ax (a, t#oax#(core.idx 0 (t#Rp) (jj - 1))))))›;
    let vdim_n = core.select (t#vdim < r, core.select (t#vdim < d, t#vdim, t#vdim + 1), r_n);
    lam retile_n (ro_m rp_m rr_m: Nat)
                 (so_m: «ro_m; Nat», sr_m: «rp_m + rr_m; Nat», vdim_m unroll_m tail_m pout_m rout_m ptail_m par_m: Nat)
                 (iota:  «rp_m + rr_m; affine.Index» → «r_n; affine.Index»,
                  omega: [«ro_n; affine.Index», «rp_m + rr_m; affine.Index»] → «ro_m; affine.Index»)
               : «so_m; t#Tp» =
        lam iota_c  (o: «rp_m + rr_m; affine.Index»): «r; affine.Index» =
            split_iota (r_n, r, d, ts) (iota o);
        lam omega_c (oo: «t#Ro; affine.Index», ov: «rp_m + rr_m; affine.Index»): «ro_m; affine.Index» =
            omega (split_omega (ro_n, t#Ro, a, ts) oo, ov);
        t#retile (ro_m, rp_m, rr_m) (so_m, sr_m, vdim_m, unroll_m, tail_m, pout_m, rout_m, ptail_m, par_m) (iota_c, omega_c);
    (t#Tp, ro_n, rp_n, t#Rr,
     split_bounds (ro_n, t#Ro, t#So, a, ts, nt), oax_n,
     split_bounds (r_n, r, t#Sr, d, ts, nt), vdim_n, t#unroll, t#tail, t#pout + (0, 1)#(d < t#pout), t#rout, ptail, par_n, retile_n);
///
/// #### split_iter_par
///
/// Split a parallel iteration dim without splitting its output axis. In contrast to split_par, neither new dim
/// writes a whole output axis, so both oax entries are the sentinel Ro. Further iteration splits remain legal.
lam split_iter_par (t: Tileable, d ts: Nat): Tileable =
    let r = t#Rp + t#Rr;
    let rp_n = t#Rp + 1;
    let r_n = rp_n + t#Rr;
    let d = refly.check (d < t#Rp, d, "split_iter: expected a parallel dim");
    let ts = refly.check (0 < ts, ts, "split_iter: tile size must be positive");
    let d = refly.check (core.bit2.or_ 2 (t#ptail == 0,
                         core.bit2.and_ 2 (t#vdim != d, t#vdim != d + 1)), d,
                         "split_iter: cannot split a partitioned parallel dim");
    let nt = check_div (t#Sr#(core.idx 0 r d), ts) / ts;
    let oax_n = ‹j: rp_n; let jj = core.bitcast Nat j;
                          core.select (core.bit2.or_ 2 (jj == d, jj == d + 1), t#Ro,
                          t#oax#(core.idx 0 (t#Rp) (jj - (0, 1)#(d < jj))))›;
    let vdim_n = t#vdim + (0, 1)#(d <= t#vdim);
    let par_n = t#par + (0, 1)#(d < t#par);
    lam retile_n (ro_m rp_m rr_m: Nat)
                 (so_m: «ro_m; Nat», sr_m: «rp_m + rr_m; Nat», vdim_m unroll_m tail_m pout_m rout_m ptail_m par_m: Nat)
                 (iota: «rp_m + rr_m; affine.Index» → «r_n; affine.Index»,
                  omega: [«t#Ro; affine.Index», «rp_m + rr_m; affine.Index»] → «ro_m; affine.Index»)
               : «so_m; t#Tp» =
        t#retile (ro_m, rp_m, rr_m) (so_m, sr_m, vdim_m, unroll_m, tail_m, pout_m, rout_m, ptail_m, par_m)
                 (comp_map (split_iota (r_n, r, d, ts), iota), omega);
    (t#Tp, t#Ro, rp_n, t#Rr, t#So, oax_n, split_bounds (r_n, r, t#Sr, d, ts, nt), vdim_n,
     t#unroll, t#tail, t#pout + (0, 1)#(d < t#pout), t#rout, t#ptail, par_n, retile_n);
///
/// #### par_extent
///
/// The extent of parallel dim `d`, checked against the output axis it writes.
lam par_extent (t: Tileable, d: Nat): Nat =
    let a = t#oax#(core.idx 0 (t#Rp) d);
    let a = refly.check (a < t#Ro, a, "strip_mine_par: dim must map to an output axis; use split_iter for reindexed dims");
    refly.check (t#So#(core.idx 0 (t#Ro) a) == t#Sr#(core.idx 0 (t#Rp + t#Rr) d),
                 t#Sr#(core.idx 0 (t#Rp + t#Rr) d),
                 "strip_mine_par: output extent (at oax#d) must match domain extent");
///
/// #### strip_mine_par
///
/// Split parallel dim `d` (d < Rp) into (N/ts, ts); `ts` must divide N. The materialized tensor is packed (see
/// `split_par`); a row-major `reshape` restores the original shape — and in a fused graph `fuse_tensor`'s read-through
/// absorbs it into the consumers' access maps.
anx lam strip_mine_par (t: Tileable, d ts: Nat): Tileable =
    split_par (t, d, ts, check_div (par_extent (t, d), ts) / ts, t#ptail);
///
/// #### partition_par
///
/// Loop partitioning of parallel dim `d` of any extent N into (⌈N/ts⌉, ts), whose last iteration of the outer half
/// runs only the remaining `N mod ts` points of the inner one (the schedule's `ptail`).
/// The schedule must make the halves `vdim − 1` and `vdim` (see `mk_sched`); the packed result is padded to whole
/// tiles, so a row-major `reshape` restores the padded shape and a `slice` the original one.
anx lam partition_par (t: Tileable, d ts: Nat): Tileable =
    let n    = par_extent (t, d);
    let tail = (n - n / ts * ts, 0)#(ts == 1);
    split_par (t, d, ts, n / ts + (0, 1)#(tail != 0), tail);
///
/// #### split_red
///
/// Split reduction dim `d` (Rp ≤ d < Rp + Rr) into two adjacent reduction dims (nt, ts) with the schedule's `tail`.
/// If the split lands inside the trailing unroll window, both halves unroll — the tap count is unchanged.
lam split_red (t: Tileable, d ts nt tail: Nat): Tileable =
    let r   = t#Rp + t#Rr;
    let r_n = t#Rp + (t#Rr + 1);
    let vdim_n   = core.select (t#vdim < r, core.select (t#vdim < d, t#vdim, t#vdim + 1), r_n);
    let par_n    = core.select (t#par < t#Rp, t#par, r_n);
    let unroll_n = core.select (d < r - t#unroll, t#unroll, t#unroll + 1);
    lam retile_n (ro_m rp_m rr_m: Nat)
                 (so_m: «ro_m; Nat», sr_m: «rp_m + rr_m; Nat», vdim_m unroll_m tail_m pout_m rout_m ptail_m par_m: Nat)
                 (iota:  «rp_m + rr_m; affine.Index» → «r_n; affine.Index»,
                  omega: [«t#Ro; affine.Index», «rp_m + rr_m; affine.Index»] → «ro_m; affine.Index»)
               : «so_m; t#Tp» =
        t#retile (ro_m, rp_m, rr_m) (so_m, sr_m, vdim_m, unroll_m, tail_m, pout_m, rout_m, ptail_m, par_m)
                 (comp_map (split_iota (r_n, r, d, ts), iota), omega);
    (t#Tp, t#Ro, t#Rp, t#Rr + 1, t#So, t#oax,
     split_bounds (r_n, r, t#Sr, d, ts, nt), vdim_n, unroll_n, tail, t#pout, t#rout, t#ptail, par_n, retile_n);
///
/// #### strip_mine_red
///
/// Split reduction dim `d` (Rp ≤ d < Rp + Rr) of extent N into two adjacent
/// reduction dims (N/ts, ts); `ts` must divide N.
anx lam strip_mine_red (t: Tileable, d ts: Nat): Tileable =
    let r = t#Rp + t#Rr;
    split_red (t, d, ts, check_div (t#Sr#(core.idx 0 r d), ts) / ts, t#tail);
///
/// #### partition_red
///
/// Loop partitioning: split the innermost reduction dim `d = Rp + Rr − 1` of any extent N into (⌈N/ts⌉, ts), whose
/// last, peeled iteration of the outer half folds only the remaining `N mod ts` points.
/// The inner half must then be the first unrolled dim and the outer half stay a loop, i.e. unroll exactly the tile:
/// `unroll_trailing (partition_red (t, d, ts), 1)`.
anx lam partition_red (t: Tileable, d ts: Nat): Tileable =
    let r = t#Rp + t#Rr;
    let n = t#Sr#(core.idx 0 r d);
    let tail = (n - n / ts * ts, 0)#(ts == 1);
    split_red (t, d, ts, n / ts + (0, 1)#(tail != 0), tail);

/// ### split_iter
///
/// Exact strip-mining of an iteration dimension, preserving output rank, shape and layout. Spatial dimensions
/// use split_iter_par; reduction dimensions use the existing reduction split. This composes with fuse_iter.
anx lam split_iter (t: Tileable, d ts: Nat): Tileable =
    let ts = refly.check (0 < ts, ts, "split_iter: tile size must be positive");
    let d = refly.check (d < t#Rp + t#Rr, d, "split_iter: dimension out of range");
    lam spatial (u: []): Tileable = split_iter_par (t, d, ts);
    lam reduction (u: []): Tileable =
        let u_lo = t#Rp + t#Rr - t#unroll;
        let d = refly.check (core.bit2.or_ 2 (t#tail == 0,
                             core.bit2.or_ 2 (d + 1 < u_lo, u_lo < d)), d,
                             "split_iter: cannot split a partitioned reduction dim");
        strip_mine_red (t, d, ts);
    (reduction, spatial)#(d < t#Rp) ();

/// ### Schedules
///
/// A `Schedule` is just a `Tileable` transformer, a **selector** a compile-time inspection of an op's
/// parameters returning the appropriate one — combinator composition, dispatched as an extract over
/// thunks so that partial evaluation resolves it entirely.
/// This is where "when is a transformation appropriate" lives: in the IR next to the op, not in a
/// lowering heuristic.
///
/// #### Schedule
///
/// A transformer of `Tileable`s.
let Schedule = Tileable → Tileable;
///
/// #### sched_id
///
lam sched_id (t: Tileable): Tileable = t;
///
/// #### unroll_budget
///
/// The greedy trailing-suffix unroll scan (the old lowering's budget scan): the number of trailing
/// reduction dims whose tap product stays within `budget`. Folded innermost-first over the
/// reduction dims with state (count, taps, alive); a dim whose extent is still open at lowering
/// time stops the scan (`if_static` residualizes its step to "over budget").
lam unroll_budget (t: Tileable, budget: Nat): Nat =
    let r = t#Rp + t#Rr;
    lam step (d: Nat, st: «3; Nat»): «3; Nat» =
        let taps  = st#1_3 * t#Sr#(core.idx 0 r d);
        let alive = st#2_3 * if_static (taps, (0, 1)#(taps <= budget), 0);
        (st#0_3 + alive, core.select (alive == 1, taps, st#1_3), alive);
    (vec.fold.r @(«3; Nat», Nat) @(t#Rr) step (‹i: t#Rr; t#Rp + core.bitcast Nat i›, (0, 1, 1)))#0_3;
///
/// #### vectorize_if
///
/// Vectorize the parallel dim `d` only when `cond` holds; otherwise the sentinel "no vector dim".
lam vectorize_if (t: Tileable, cond: Bool, d: Nat): Tileable =
    vectorize (t, (t#Rp + t#Rr, d)#cond);
///
/// #### conv_schedule
///
/// The convolution schedule, selected from the op's parameters — the old lowering's heuristics,
/// now plain IR next to the op:
///
/// - unroll the trailing reduction dims greedily while their tap product fits the register budget
///   (the kh·kw window; a small cin joins it) — constant tap offsets, no window loops;
/// - run `ow` (parallel dim 3) innermost with unit stride over a row of accumulators iff it beats
///   the innermost *remaining* reduction dim on unit-stride/broadcast reads: ow reads the padded
///   input at stride_w and is broadcast in the weight (score [stride_w = 1] + 1); an un-unrolled
///   kw reads the input at dilation_w and the weight at unit stride ([dilation_w = 1] + 1 — the
///   classic nest is already unit-stride, so vectorizing buys nothing); kh/cin score 0; with the
///   whole reduction unrolled there is nothing to beat.
///
/// Open extents/strides residualize to no unrolling and no vector dim (`if_static`).
/// `conv_rule` applies it to any windowed kernel (see `Kernel`), reading the w strides off the kernel.
lam conv_schedule (stride_w dilation_w: Nat) (t: Tileable): Tileable =
    let u     = unroll_budget (t, 32);
    let s_ow  = (0, 1)#(stride_w == 1) + 1;
    let s_red = core.select (u == 0, (0, 1)#(dilation_w == 1) + 1, core.select (u < t#Rr, 0, s_ow));
    let ow    = t#Sr#(core.idx 0 (t#Rp + t#Rr) 3);
    let vec   = if_static (stride_w * dilation_w * ow, s_red < s_ow, ff);
    // Rows too short for the vectorizer (ow < 16) are processed in BLOCKS: strip-mine oh by the
    // largest block size ≤ 4 that divides it and sink both halves (vdim = ro − 2 selects mr_nest's
    // blocked branch, which jams the block dim into the vector body — one reduction pass folds the
    // whole row block, sharing the hoisted window weights). Otherwise the split is the neutral
    // ts = 1 (its size-1 axis folds away), so one code path serves every case and no divisibility
    // check fires on an untaken choice.
    let oh    = t#Sr#(core.idx 0 (t#Rp + t#Rr) 2);
    lam divides (d: Nat): Bool = oh / d * d == oh;
    let bs    = ((1, 2)#(divides 2), 4)#(divides 4);
    let block = if_static (oh * ow, core.bit2.and_ 2 (vec, core.bit2.and_ 2 (ow < 16, bs != 1)), ff);
    unroll_trailing (vectorize_if (strip_mine_par (t, 2, (1, bs)#block), vec, (4, 3)#block), u);
///
/// #### dot_tile
///
/// The contraction tile `dot_schedule` register-blocks by: 4 or else 3 if it divides `K`, else `1`,
/// whereupon a known `K ≥ 4` is partitioned by 4 instead (see `partition_red`).
/// A tile of 2 is slower than partitioning by 4.
lam dot_tile (K: Nat): Nat =
    ((1, 3)#(K / 3 * 3 == K), 4)#(K / 4 * 4 == K);
///
/// #### dot_k
///
/// The contraction part of `dot_schedule`: the innermost contraction register-blocked by `dot_tile`, else partitioned by
/// 4, the tile unrolled.
lam dot_k (t: Tileable): Tileable =
    let r   = t#Rp + t#Rr;
    let d   = r - 1;
    let K   = t#Sr#(core.idx 0 r d);
    let ts0 = dot_tile K;
    let ts  = if_static (ts0, ts0, 1);
    // A known `K ≥ 4` that no tile divides is partitioned by 4; a dividing tile partitions without a tail.
    let ts  = (ts, 4)#(if_static (K, core.bit2.and_ 2 (4 <= K, ts0 == 1), ff));
    unroll_trailing (partition_red (t, d, ts), 1);
///
/// #### dot_schedule
///
/// The dot-product schedule for a contraction whose right operand stores the last output dim
/// unit-stride (\f$A \cdot B\f$): that dim is invariant in the left operand, so it runs innermost over a row of
/// accumulators (GEMM-style vectorization) and the innermost contraction is register-blocked —
/// strip-mined by `dot_tile` or else partitioned by 4, the tile unrolled.
///
/// - Blocking only needs the **divisibility** decided, which is weaker than a literal extent: a
///   factored extent (`«(n+3)/4*4, …»`, say a padded row) folds the guard to `tt` under a symbolic
///   `n`, because `core.nat` cancels `(c*x)/c`.
/// - `if_static` therefore gates on the tile size rather than on `K` — a tile that folded to a
///   literal is decided, no matter which kind of extent decided it.
/// - An undecided divisibility takes the neutral tile (`ts = 1`), so one selector serves static,
///   symbolic and runtime shapes.
lam dot_schedule (r_out: Nat) (t: Tileable): Tileable = dot_k (vectorize (t, r_out - 1));
///
/// #### dot_rb_rows
///
/// The rows of a register block over `m` rows, or `0` for none: 6 or else 4, as many as the target's `acc_regs` hold
/// the 16 32-bit accumulators of (6 x 16 blocks keep 12 of AVX2's 16 vector registers, 4 x 16 blocks 16 of NEON's 32).
/// Where the rows do not divide `m`, the last block is partial (`partition_par`) if `tail_ok`.
lam dot_rb_rows (m: Nat, tail_ok: Bool): Nat =
    let cap = target.acc_regs () / ((64 + target.vec_bytes () - 1) / target.vec_bytes ());
    lam fits (r: Nat): Bool = core.bit2.and_ 2 (r <= cap, core.bit2.or_ 2 (core.bit2.and_ 2 (m / r * r == m, r < m),
                                                                           core.bit2.and_ 2 (tail_ok, 2 * r <= m)));
    ((0, 4)#(fits 4), 6)#(fits 6);
///
/// #### dot_schedule_rb
///
/// `dot_schedule` with the last two output dims (M, N), 16 | N, register-blocked by `rows` x 16 (see `dot_rb_rows`): the
/// block's accumulators stay in registers across the whole contraction (`btensor.mr_nest`'s register-blocked nest),
/// each load of the right operand serving `rows` rows.
/// The blocks run in the order (N/16, M/rows), so the right operand's k x 16 column stays in cache across all of M.
lam dot_schedule_rb (r_out rows: Nat) (t: Tileable): Tileable =
    let q    = r_out - 2;
    let t1   = partition_par (strip_mine_par (t, r_out - 1, 16), q, rows);
    let r1   = t1#2_15 + t1#3_15;
    let perm = ‹j: r1; let jj = core.bitcast Nat j;
                       core.select (jj == q, q + 2, core.select (jj == q + 1, q, core.select (jj == q + 2, q + 1, jj)))›;
    dot_k (vectorize (interchange (t1, perm), r_out));
///
/// #### dot_pack_m
///
/// The fewest left-operand rows for which `dot_product_impl` packs the right operand.
let dot_pack_m = 48;
///
/// #### dot_pack_k
///
/// The shortest contraction for which `dot_product_impl` packs the right operand.
let dot_pack_k = 128;
///
/// #### dot_nc
///
/// The cache block of the last output dim `n` for `dot_trace`: the largest of 512, 256, …, 16 that divides
/// `n`, stays below it and keeps the right operand's `k × Nc` panel within the target's `cache_elems`, else `0`.
lam dot_nc (n k: Nat): Nat =
    lam ok (c: Nat): Bool = core.bit2.and_ 2 (n / c * c == n, core.bit2.and_ 2 (c < n, c * k <= target.cache_elems ()));
    ((((((0, 16)#(ok 16), 32)#(ok 32), 64)#(ok 64), 128)#(ok 128), 256)#(ok 256), 512)#(ok 512);
///
/// #### dot_kb
///
/// The contraction steps per cached panel: the largest divisor of `kq` below it whose `w` elements per step stay
/// within the target's `cache_elems`, else `kq`.
lam dot_kb (kq w: Nat): Nat =
    let bmax = target.cache_elems () / w;
    lam pick (b acc: Nat): Nat = (acc, b)#(core.bit2.and_ 2 (kq / b * b == kq, core.bit2.and_ 2 (b <= bmax, b < kq)));
    vec.fold.r @(Nat, Nat) @64 pick (‹i: 64; 64 - core.bitcast Nat i›, kq);
///
/// #### dot_nblk
///
/// The N block of `dot_trace`, its loop moved outside the left free dims.
lam dot_nblk (r_out nb na nc: Nat) (t: Tileable): Tileable =
    let t1   = strip_mine_par (t, r_out - 1, nc);
    let r1   = t1#2_15 + t1#3_15;
    let perm = ‹j: r1; let jj = core.bitcast Nat j;
                       core.select (jj < nb, jj,
                       core.select (jj < r_out - na, jj + na,
                       core.select (jj < r_out, jj + nb - (r_out - na), jj)))›;
    interchange (t1, perm);
///
/// #### dot_order
///
/// The loop order of `dot_nblk` without its split: the right operand's free dims but the last run outside the left
/// operand's `na` free dims.
lam dot_order (r_out nb na: Nat) (t: Tileable): Tileable =
    let r    = t#Rp + t#Rr;
    let q    = r_out - 1;
    let perm = ‹j: r; let jj = core.bitcast Nat j;
                      core.select (jj < nb, jj,
                      core.select (jj < q - na, jj + na,
                      core.select (jj < q, jj + nb - (q - na), jj)))›;
    interchange (t, perm);
///
/// #### dot_pack_nc
///
/// The N block a packed right operand is laid out in (see `dot_product_impl`), or `0` not to pack: the width of a
/// register block, for at least `dot_pack_m` rows and `dot_pack_k` contraction steps to amortize the copy, and a last
/// left free dim of `m_rb` rows that register blocks cover (see `dot_rb_rows`, with `tail_ok`).
/// `N` need not be a multiple of the width: `dot_product_impl` pads the last panel.
lam dot_pack_nc (m m_rb k n: Nat, tail_ok: Bool): Nat =
    let ok = core.bit2.and_ 2 (16 < n, core.bit2.and_ 2 (dot_pack_m <= m,
             core.bit2.and_ 2 (dot_pack_k <= k, dot_rb_rows (m_rb, tail_ok) != 0)));
    (0, 16)#ok;
///
/// #### dot_kblk
///
/// The K block of `dot_trace`: its reductions are (K/ts, ts) for a single contraction; block K/ts by kb.
lam dot_kblk (r_out na nc: Nat) (t2: Tileable): Tileable =
    let r2  = t2#Rp + t2#Rr;
    let kq  = t2#Sr#(core.idx 0 r2 (r2 - 2));
    let ts  = t2#Sr#(core.idx 0 r2 (r2 - 1));
    let kb  = dot_kb (kq, ts * nc);
    let blk = if_static (kq * ts, core.bit2.and_ 2 (t2#Rr == 2, core.bit2.and_ 2 (t2#tail == 0,
              core.bit2.and_ 2 (target.cache_elems () < kq * ts * nc, core.bit2.and_ 2 (4 <= kb, kb < kq)))), ff);
    hoist_red (strip_mine_red (t2, r2 - 2, (kq, kb)#blk), r_out - na, (0, 1)#blk);
///
/// #### dot_trace
///
/// The one schedule of an A·B contraction, its tiles the parameters (`dot_params` picks them for a kernel):
///
/// - `nc`, the cache block of the last output dim N (`dot_nblk`), its block loop outside the left operand's free dims
///   so the right operand's `k × nc` panel stays in cache for all of them; `0` for none;
/// - `rows`, the register blocks of `rows` x 16 over M and N (`dot_schedule_rb`), whose accumulators stay in registers
///   across the contraction; `0` for `dot_schedule`'s vector loop over N;
/// - `kblk`, whether the contraction is cache-blocked too (`dot_kblk`), its blocks outside the left free dims;
/// - `packed`, a right operand `cache_read` into panels: its N blocks are a free dim already, ordered outside the left
///   free dims (`dot_order`) instead of split, the K block sized by the panel width.
///
/// Each step selects its transformation before applying it: the Tileables of the two choices differ in rank.
lam dot_trace (nc rows: Nat, kblk packed: Bool) (r_out nb na: Nat) (t: Tileable): Tileable =
    let blk  = core.bit2.and_ 2 (nc != 0, (tt, ff)#packed);
    let t1   = ((sched_id, dot_nblk (r_out, nb, na, nc))#blk, dot_order (r_out, nb, na))#packed t;
    let r1   = r_out + (0, 1)#blk;
    let t2   = (dot_schedule r1, dot_schedule_rb (r1, rows))#(rows != 0) t1;
    let nc_k = (nc, t#So#(core.idx 0 (t#Ro) (t#Ro - 1)))#packed;
    (sched_id, dot_kblk ((r_out, r_out - 1)#packed, na, nc_k))#kblk t2;
///
/// #### dot_schedule_kvec
///
/// The dot-product schedule for a contraction whose right operand stores the **contraction**
/// unit-stride per output cell (\f$x \cdot W^T\f$ — the fc/linear layout, where `fuse_tensor`'s read-through
/// absorbs the weight transpose into the access map).
/// Vectorizing the last output dim would read a different weight row per lane (gathers), so instead
///
/// - the contraction itself runs as the vector loop — unit-stride in both the input row and each
///   weight row, folded with a horizontal sum (see `btensor.mr_nest`'s reduction-vectorized branch,
///   selected by a `vdim` naming the innermost reduction dim, where `unroll` names the jam width);
/// - a 2-D block of output cells is unroll-and-jammed into its body over independent vector
///   accumulators: the last output dim strip-mined by the largest divisor ≤ 4 (jammed lanes share the
///   input-row loads) × the second-to-last by 2 (jammed rows share the weight-row loads — without
///   this the whole weight matrix re-streams once per batch row).
///
/// An extent still open at lowering time, an indivisible one, and the missing row dim of a rank-1
/// output all take the neutral split (`1`), whose size-1 jam dim folds away.
lam dot_schedule_kvec (r_out: Nat) (t: Tileable): Tileable =
    // A block counts only when it divides AND leaves a non-trivial outer dim (`b < k`): an exact
    // split's leading size-1 axis would type-collapse the packed output («1; T» ≡ T).
    lam blk_by (k b: Nat): Bool = core.bit2.and_ 2 (k / b * b == k, b < k);
    let d   = r_out - 1;
    let n   = t#So#(core.idx 0 (t#Ro) d);
    let bs  = if_static (n, ((1, 2)#(blk_by (n, 2)), 4)#(blk_by (n, 4)), 1);
    let t2  = strip_mine_par (t, d, bs);
    let d2  = r_out - 2;
    let m   = t#So#(core.idx 0 (t#Ro) d2);
    let ms  = (1, if_static (m, (1, 2)#(blk_by (m, 2)), 1))#(2 <= r_out);
    let t3  = strip_mine_par (t2, d2, ms);
    // Dims now (…, m/ms, ms, n/bs, bs | red): swap ms ↔ n/bs so both jam dims are the trailing
    // parallel pair. Identity for a rank-1 output, where the neutral split already sits next to
    // `bs` ((n/bs, 1, bs | red)). `r3` is spelled from t3's own (positional) fields so the pack's
    // length is definitionally the «t#Rp + t#Rr» the dependent `interchange` domain expects —
    // `core.nat.add` does not reassociate an equivalent spelling.
    let r3   = t3#2_15 + t3#3_15;
    let swap = 2 <= r_out;
    let pa   = r_out - 1;
    let pb   = r_out;
    let perm = ‹j: r3; let jj = core.bitcast Nat j;
                       core.select (core.bit2.and_ 2 (swap, jj == pa), pb,
                       core.select (core.bit2.and_ 2 (swap, jj == pb), pa, jj))›;
    unroll_trailing (vectorize (interchange (t3, perm), r3 - 1), 2);
///
/// #### pool_schedule
///
/// The pooling schedule: unroll the window while it stays within budget. Pooling reads its single
/// input in every loop dim (no broadcast operand), so `ow` scores [stride_w = 1] against an
/// un-unrolled kw's [dilation_w = 1] — the row accumulator never wins and the nest stays classic.
lam pool_schedule (stride dilation: «2; Nat») (t: Tileable): Tileable =
    let u     = unroll_budget (t, 32);
    let s_ow  = (0, 1)#(stride#1_2 == 1);
    let s_red = core.select (u == 0, (0, 1)#(dilation#1_2 == 1), core.select (u < t#Rr, 0, s_ow));
    let ow    = t#Sr#(core.idx 0 (t#Rp + t#Rr) 3);
    let vec   = if_static (stride#1_2 * dilation#1_2 * ow, s_red < s_ow, ff);
    unroll_trailing (vectorize_if (t, vec, 3), u);
///
/// ## Implementations
///
/// The `_impl` annexes `lower_tensor` re-applies to expand the derived operations above, together
/// with the fold steps and access maps they read through.
/// Most are a single `map_reduce` over a copy step — the operation itself lives in the access map.
/// They are `priv`: the phase resolves them through the annex table, so no path from outside this
/// plugin reaches them and the axioms above stay the only public spelling of these operations.
///
/// The declaration order is load-bearing beyond the usual definition-before-use: `lower_tensor`
/// resolves an `_impl` in the world it is *building*, so an impl must be declared before any annex
/// whose body applies the axiom it implements — `reshape_impl` before `conv_impl` and
/// `dot_product_impl`, which reshape their packed results.
///
/// ### tensor_copy
///
/// Ignores the (⊥) accumulator and returns the read element — the fold step of every pure
/// re-indexing operation below.
fun tensor_copy {T: *} (acc: T, y: T)@tt: T = return y;
///
/// ### bid_map
///
/// `o ↦ (o#(index#i) · [s_in#i ≠ 1])_i`: input axis `i` reads output-loop var `o#(index#i)` (the transpose), unless
/// it is a broadcast axis (`s_in#i = 1`), which reads index 0.
lam bid_map {r_in r_out: Nat} (s_in: «r_in; Nat», index: «r_in; Idx r_out»)
            (o: «r_out; affine.Index»): «r_in; affine.Index» =
    ‹i: r_in; affine.semiop.mul (o#(index#i), core.select (s_in#i == 1, 0, 1))›;
///
/// ### broadcast_in_dim_impl

priv anx lam broadcast_in_dim_impl {T: *, r_in r_out: Nat}
    (s_in: «r_in; Nat», s_out: «r_out; Nat», input: «s_in; T», index: «r_in; Idx r_out»): «s_out; T» =
    map_reduce (s_out, s_out) (tensor_copy, ⊥: T)
        affine.id (bid_map (s_in, index),) input;
///
/// ### flip_map
///
/// `o ↦ ((s_in#d − 1) − o#d)_d`: the reverse read map.
lam flip_map {r: Nat} (s_in: «r; Nat») (o: «r; affine.Index»): «r; affine.Index» =
    ‹d: r; affine.op.sub (affine.lit (s_in#d - 1), o#d)›;
///
/// ### flip_impl

priv anx lam flip_impl {T: *, r: Nat} (s_in: «r; Nat») (input: «s_in; T»): «s_in; T» =
    map_reduce (s_in, s_in) (tensor_copy, ⊥: T)
        affine.id (flip_map s_in,) input;
///
/// ### gather_pointwise_elem_impl
///
/// One output element: reads `index` at the output coordinate, then the source at that coordinate
/// with axis `dim` replaced by the loaded value.
priv anx lam gather_pointwise_elem_impl {T: *, r: Nat}
                                   (s_src: «r; Nat», s_idx: «r; Nat»)
                                   (dim: Idx r)
                                   (out_indices: «r; I64»)
                                   (input: «s_src; T», index: «s_idx; Idx (s_src#dim)»): T =
    let index_coords = ‹d: r; core.conv.u (s_idx#d) (out_indices#d)›;
    let source_axis_index = core.bitcast I64 (index#index_coords);
    let source_coords = ‹d: r;
        let coordinate = core.select (core.icmp.e @r (d, dim), source_axis_index, out_indices#d);
        core.conv.u (s_src#d) coordinate›;
    input#source_coords;
///
/// ### map_impl
///
/// A `map_reduce` whose fold step drops the accumulator and applies `app` to the element tuple.
priv anx lam map_impl {T: *, ni: Nat, Is: «ni; *»}
                     (app: «i: ni; Is#i» → T)
                     {r: Nat, s: «r; Nat»}
                     (is: «i: ni; «s; Is#i» »)
                     : «s; T» =
  fun app_mr (x: T, y: «i: ni; Is#i»)@tt = return (app y);
  // The `{Tis, Ris, Sis}` group stays explicit: with a *symbolic* `ni`, solving `Sis#i ≡ s` for all `i`
  // (i.e. `Sis := ‹ni; s›`) is Miller pattern unification. Adding that rule to `Checker::alpha_impl_` was tried
  // and reverted — even with an occurs check it fires inside `tuple`'s machinery and commits a wrong solution.
  map_reduce (s, s) @(Is, ‹ni; r›, ‹ni; s›) (app_mr, ⊥: T)
      // `‹ni; …›` builds the pack bottom-up, so there is no expected type to drive implicit insertion for the
      // element; `affine.id` needs its rank here.
      affine.id ‹ni; affine.id @r› is;
///
/// ### pool_fun
///
/// Wraps a binary fold `g` as a `map_reduce` reduction `Fn [T, «1; T»] → T`.
pub fun pool_fun {T: *} (g: [T, T] → T) (acc y: T)@tt: T = return (g (acc, y));
///
/// ### reduce_shape
///
/// `s` without axis `ax`.
pub lam reduce_shape {r: Nat} (ax: Idx r, s: «r; Nat»): «r - 1; Nat» =
    ‹i: r - 1; s#(core.idx 0 r (shift_ax (core.bitcast Nat ax, core.bitcast Nat i)))›;
///
/// ### reduce
///
/// Folds `input` along axis `ax` with `g`, seeded with `init`; the result drops that axis.
/// The output axes run as the leading loops, `ax` as the trailing reduction loop.
pub lam reduce {T: *} (g: [T, T] → T, init: T) {r: Nat} (ax: Idx r) {s: «r; Nat»} (input: «s; T»): «reduce_shape (ax, s); T» =
    let a = core.bitcast Nat ax;
    map_reduce (reduce_shape (ax, s), ‹i: r; let ii = core.bitcast Nat i; s#(core.idx 0 r (core.select (ii < r - 1, shift_ax (a, ii), a)))›)
        (pool_fun g, init)
        (proj_map ‹i: r - 1; core.bitcast Nat i›)
        (proj_map ‹j: r; let jj = core.bitcast Nat j; core.select (jj < a, jj, core.select (jj == a, r - 1, jj - 1))›)
        input;
///
/// ### binary_ax
///
/// `binary app (x, y)` where `y` lacks axis `ax` of `x` and is broadcast along it.
pub lam binary_ax {Ti1 Ti2 To: *} (app: [Ti1, Ti2] → To) {r: Nat} (ax: Idx r) {s: «r; Nat»}
                  (x: «s; Ti1», y: «reduce_shape (ax, s); Ti2»): «s; To» =
    fun app_mr (_: To, (u: Ti1, v: Ti2))@tt: To = return (app (u, v));
    let a = core.bitcast Nat ax;
    map_reduce (s, s) @((Ti1, Ti2), (r, r - 1), (s, reduce_shape (ax, s))) (app_mr, ⊥: To)
        affine.id (affine.id @r, proj_map ‹i: r - 1; shift_ax (a, core.bitcast Nat i)›) (x, y);
///
/// ### softmax
///
/// Numerically stable softmax along axis `ax`: `exp (x - max) / Σ exp (x - max)`.
pub lam softmax (m: Nat) {pe: «2; Nat»} {r: Nat} (ax: Idx r) {s: «r; Nat»} (input: «s; math.F pe»): «s; math.F pe» =
    let F  = math.F pe;
    let mx = reduce (math.extrema.fmax m, math.neg_inf pe) ax input;
    let e  = binary_ax (λ (x y: F): F = math.exp.exp m (math.arith.sub m (x, y))) ax (input, mx);
    binary_ax (math.arith.div m) ax (e, reduce (math.arith.add m, 0:F) ax e);
///
/// ### pool2d_in_map
///
/// Windowed input read over the loop vector `(n, c, oh, ow, kh, kw)`:
/// `(n, c, stride#0·oh + dilation#0·kh, stride#1·ow + dilation#1·kw)`.
lam pool2d_in_map (stride dilation: «2; Nat») (o: «6; affine.Index»): «4; affine.Index» =
    (o#0_6, o#1_6,
     affine.op.add (affine.semiop.mul (o#2_6, stride#0_2), affine.semiop.mul (o#4_6, dilation#0_2)),
     affine.op.add (affine.semiop.mul (o#3_6, stride#1_2), affine.semiop.mul (o#5_6, dilation#1_2)));
///
/// ### pool_impl

priv anx lam pool_impl {T: *} (g: [T, T] → T, init: T) {n c h w: Nat}
                      (kernel stride dilation padding s_out: «2; Nat»)
                      (input: «n, c, h, w; T»)
                    : «n, c, s_out#0_2, s_out#1_2; T» =
    let s_in     = (n, c, h, w);
    let pad_lohi = (0, 0, padding#0_2, padding#1_2);
    let padded   = pad s_in (0, pad_lohi, pad_lohi) (input, init);
    let s_padded = shape 4 padded;

    // Schedule (frontend, selected from the parameters — see `pool_schedule`).
    materialize (pool_schedule (stride, dilation) (
        mr_tileable @1 @(4, 2)
            ((n, c, s_out#0_2, s_out#1_2), (0, 1, 2, 3), (n, c, s_out#0_2, s_out#1_2, kernel#0_2, kernel#1_2))
            @(T, 4, s_padded)
            (pool_fun g, init, id @T)
            (proj_map @(6, 4) (0, 1, 2, 3))
            (pool2d_in_map (stride, dilation),)
            padded));
///
/// ### repeat_map
///
/// `o ↦ (o#d mod s_in#d)_d`: the wrap-around (tiling) read map.
lam repeat_map {r: Nat} (s_in: «r; Nat») (o: «r; affine.Index»): «r; affine.Index» =
    ‹d: r; affine.semiop.rem (o#d, s_in#d)›;
///
/// ### repeat_impl

priv anx lam repeat_impl {T: *, r: Nat} (s_in: «r; Nat») (s_out: «r; Nat») (input: «s_in; T»): «s_out; T» =
    map_reduce (s_out, s_out) (tensor_copy, ⊥: T)
        affine.id (repeat_map s_in,) input;
///
/// ### reshape_map
///
/// `o ↦ delinearize(linearize(o, s_out), s_in)`: the row-major output→input index map.
anx lam reshape_map {r_in r_out: Nat} (s_in: «r_in; Nat», s_out: «r_out; Nat»)
                (o: «r_out; affine.Index»): «r_in; affine.Index» =
    affine.delinearize (affine.linearize (o, s_out), s_in);
///
/// ### reshape_impl

priv anx lam reshape_impl {T: *, r_in r_out: Nat}
                         (s_in: «r_in; Nat») (s_out: «r_out; Nat») (input: «s_in; T»): «s_out; T» =
    map_reduce (s_out, s_out) (tensor_copy, ⊥: T)
        affine.id (reshape_map (s_in, s_out),) input;
///
/// ### conv_fun
///
/// Fused multiply-add over the ring `R`; `ab#0` is the padded-input element, `ab#1` the weight.
fun conv_fun (R: Ring) (acc: R#T, ab: «2; R#T»)@tt: R#T = return (R#add (acc, R#mul (ab#0_2, ab#1_2)));
///
/// ### conv2d_in_map
///
/// The `«7; affine.Index»` loop vector is `o = (n, co, oh, ow, ci, kh, kw)`, so the weight read map
/// `(co, ci, kh, kw)` and the output write map `(n, co, oh, ow)` are `proj_map` projections of it;
/// only this padded-input read needs arithmetic:
/// `(n, ci, stride#0·oh + dilation#0·kh, stride#1·ow + dilation#1·kw)`.
lam conv2d_in_map (stride dilation: «2; Nat») (o: «7; affine.Index»): «4; affine.Index» =
    (o#0_7, o#4_7,
     affine.op.add (affine.semiop.mul (o#2_7, stride#0_2), affine.semiop.mul (o#5_7, dilation#0_2)),
     affine.op.add (affine.semiop.mul (o#3_7, stride#1_2), affine.semiop.mul (o#6_7, dilation#1_2)));
///
/// ### Schedule rules
///
/// A kernel is scheduled by the structure of its loop dims, not by its op (cf. TVM's dlight): the first `SchedRule`
/// whose `applies` holds for the kernel schedules it (`pick_rule`), else the fallback.
///
/// #### Kernel
///
/// The structure of a two-operand kernel over its Tileable's loop dims `0 … Rp+Rr−1`: `reads (i, d)`, whether input `i`
/// reads loop dim `d`, and `unit (i, d)`, the coefficient with which `d` drives the unit-stride axis of input `i`'s
/// storage, `0` where it does not — e.g. a contraction `C[S, I, J] = Σ_K A[S, I, K] ⋆ B[S, J, K]` has its S dims read by
/// both inputs, its I dims by `A` only, its J dims by `B` only; a window reads one axis with several dims.
/// The context besides: whether the kernel is a `compute_at` producer or consumer (`plain`) and whether its right
/// operand is `cache_read` into panels, its last J dim the lanes (`packed`), and whether those panels are built
/// once before any parallel consumer (`shared`).
let Kernel = [reads: [Nat, Nat] → Bool, unit: [Nat, Nat] → Nat, plain packed shared: Bool];
///
/// The number of loop dims in `[lo, hi)` satisfying `p`.
lam kernel_count (lo hi: Nat) (p: Nat → Bool): Nat =
    vec.fold.r @(Nat, Nat) @(hi - lo) (lm (d acc: Nat): Nat = acc + (0, 1)#(p d)) (‹i: hi - lo; lo + core.bitcast Nat i›, 0);
/// The S dims (read by both inputs) and I dims (by the left one only) among the parallel dims; a contraction's domain
/// orders them S, I, J, K.
lam kernel_ns (k: Kernel, t: Tileable): Nat =
    kernel_count (0, t#Rp) (lm (d: Nat): Bool = core.bit2.and_ 2 (k#reads (0, d), k#reads (1, d)));
lam kernel_ni (k: Kernel, t: Tileable): Nat =
    kernel_count (0, t#Rp) (lm (d: Nat): Bool = core.bit2.and_ 2 (k#reads (0, d), (tt, ff)#(k#reads (1, d))));
/// Whether a reduction dim is the right operand's unit-stride axis (x·Wᵀ).
lam kernel_kvec (k: Kernel, t: Tileable): Bool =
    kernel_count (t#Rp, t#Rp + t#Rr) (lm (d: Nat): Bool = k#unit (1, d) == 1) != 0;
/// Whether the left operand's unit-stride axis is driven by more than one loop dim: a window.
lam kernel_windowed (k: Kernel, t: Tileable): Bool =
    2 <= kernel_count (0, t#Rp + t#Rr) (lm (d: Nat): Bool = k#unit (0, d) != 0);
///
/// #### SchedRule
///
/// `rows` are the rows of the schedule's register blocks, whose partition pads M to whole blocks (see `unpack` in
/// `dot_contract`), `0` where it does not partition.
let SchedRule = [applies: [Kernel, Tileable] → Bool, rows: [Kernel, Tileable] → Nat, sched: [Kernel, Tileable] → Tileable];
/// #### pick_rule
///
/// The index of the first `tt` among the rules' `applies`, `n` for none.
/// Decided here once every entry is a literal; `tensor::Lower` decides the rest after it has instantiated the kernel,
/// an entry still open then not applying — the rules themselves must stay unapplied until then, since their
/// `if_static` guards would be residualized to their defaults in a generic body.
axm pick_rule: {n: Nat} → «n; Bool» → Nat, normalize_pick_rule;
///
/// #### schedule_kernel
///
/// Schedules a kernel by the first rule that applies, else the fallback, and materializes it.
/// The result of a schedule whose partitioned dims pad it (see `partition_par`, `cache_read`) is the row-major prefix of
/// the padded shape: `a_m` is the final axis a rule's `rows` partition pads, the last axis is what the result's size
/// leaves to it, and `s_fin` merges the trailing output axes of the domain.
lam schedule_kernel {T: *} {n: Nat} (rules: «n; SchedRule», fallback: SchedRule) {r_fin: Nat} (s_fin: «r_fin; Nat», a_m: Nat)
                    (k: Kernel) (t: Tileable): «s_fin; T» =
    let i     = pick_rule @n ‹j: n; (rules#j)#applies (k, t)›;
    let sr    = (rules#(core.idx 0 n (core.select (i == n, 0, i))), fallback)#(i == n);
    let sched = sr#sched (k, t);
    lam unpack (rows: Nat) {r_p: Nat} (so: «r_p; Nat») (x: «so; T»): «s_fin; T» =
        lam size {r: Nat} (s: «r; Nat»): Nat = vec.fold.r @(Nat, Nat) @r (lm (p acc: Nat): Nat = p * acc) (s, 1);
        let m     = s_fin#(core.idx 0 r_fin a_m);
        let m_pad = (m, (m + rows - 1) / rows * rows)#(rows != 0);
        let n     = s_fin#(core.idx 0 r_fin (r_fin - 1));
        let s_m   = ‹j: r_fin; core.select (core.bitcast Nat j == a_m, m_pad, s_fin#j)›;
        let n_pad = size so / (size s_m / n);
        let s_pad = ‹j: r_fin; core.select (core.bitcast Nat j == r_fin - 1, n_pad, s_m#j)›;
        let prefix = map_reduce (s_fin, s_fin) (tensor_copy, ⊥: T) affine.id (reshape_map (so, s_pad),) x;
        // Every split adds a dim, so a result of the final rank is the final shape itself; stated as a cast, since the
        // identity reshape over an opaque rule's shape does not fold. Padding needs static extents (see `dot_static`).
        let same = (reshape so s_fin x, core.bitcast «s_fin; T» x)#(r_p == r_fin);
        (same, prefix)#(if_static (size so * size s_fin, core.bit2.or_ 2 (m_pad != m, n_pad != n), ff));
    // The rule is generic over the kernel, so the checker cannot see its schedule's element type is `T`.
    unpack (sr#rows (k, t)) (sched#4_15) (core.bitcast «sched#4_15; T» (materialize sched));
///
/// #### Contraction kernels
///
/// The extents a contraction's rules decide by: M, the left operand's last free dim (its first dim where it has none),
/// N, the last output dim, and K, the product of the contractions.
lam kernel_m (k: Kernel, t: Tileable): Nat =
    t#So#(core.idx 0 (t#Ro) ((0, kernel_ns (k, t) + kernel_ni (k, t) - 1)#(1 <= kernel_ni (k, t))));
lam kernel_n (t: Tileable): Nat = t#So#(core.idx 0 (t#Ro) (t#Ro - 1));
lam kernel_k (t: Tileable): Nat =
    vec.fold.r @(Nat, Nat) @(t#Rr) (lm (p acc: Nat): Nat = p * acc) (‹i: t#Rr; t#Sr#(core.idx 0 (t#Rp + t#Rr) (t#Rp + core.bitcast Nat i))›, 1);
/// Whether M can run as `rows` x 16 register blocks against an N of `n`.
lam kernel_rb (k: Kernel, t: Tileable) (rows n: Nat): Bool =
    core.bit2.and_ 2 (1 <= kernel_ni (k, t), core.bit2.and_ 2 (rows != 0, core.bit2.and_ 2 (n / 16 * 16 == n, 16 < n)));
/// The rows of a register block: a partial last block only where M is the outermost output dim (see `dot_rb_rows`).
/// The N cache block (see `dot_nc`): above K = 256 the contraction is cache-blocked itself.
///
/// x·Wᵀ: the contraction as the vector loop; plain, without the jam blocks, whose strip-mining packs the output.
lam dot_rule_kvec_plain (k: Kernel, t: Tileable): Tileable = vectorize (t, t#Rp + t#Rr - 1);
lam dot_rule_kvec_plain_ok (k: Kernel, t: Tileable): Bool = core.bit2.and_ 2 (kernel_kvec (k, t), k#plain);
lam dot_rule_kvec (k: Kernel, t: Tileable): Tileable = dot_schedule_kvec (t#Ro) t;
lam dot_rule_kvec_ok (k: Kernel, t: Tileable): Bool = kernel_kvec (k, t);
lam dot_rows_none (k: Kernel, t: Tileable): Nat = 0;
/// The N block of `dot_nc` (above K = 256 the contraction is cache-blocked itself) and, where no K block will be, the
/// one sized by the whole contraction, so the whole `k × nc` panel fits.
lam dot_ncb (k: Kernel, t: Tileable): Nat =
    let kk   = kernel_k t;
    let nc_0 = dot_nc (kernel_n t, (256, kk)#(kk < 256));
    (if_static (nc_0, (0, nc_0)#(kernel_ns (k, t) + kernel_ni (k, t) < t#Ro), 0), 0)#(k#plain);
lam dot_nc_rb (k: Kernel, t: Tileable): Nat = if_static (dot_ncb (k, t), dot_nc (kernel_n t, kernel_k t), 0);
/// Cache and register blocks exist only for static extents: a case over them is `ff` for symbolic ones.
lam dot_static (k: Kernel, t: Tileable) (c: Bool): Bool = if_static (kernel_m (k, t) * kernel_n t * kernel_k t, c, ff);
/// The rows of the register blocks: a partial last block only where M is the outermost output dim (see `dot_rb_rows`),
/// none inside a K block.
lam dot_rows (k: Kernel, t: Tileable): Nat =
    dot_rb_rows (kernel_m (k, t), core.bit2.and_ 2 (kernel_ns (k, t) == 0, kernel_ni (k, t) == 1));
lam dot_rows_k (k: Kernel, t: Tileable): Nat = dot_rb_rows (kernel_m (k, t), ff);
///
/// #### dot_params
///
/// The tiles `(nc, rows, kblk)` of `dot_trace` for a kernel, by the first case that applies: a packed right operand; an
/// N block the register blocks fit in, sized by the whole contraction; an N block with the register blocks inside its
/// K block; an N block alone; register blocks alone, for a right operand with N as its only free dim; none.
/// A case still open once the kernel is built is decided by tensor::Lower (`pick_rule`).
lam dot_params (k: Kernel, t: Tileable): [Nat, Nat, Bool] =
    let ncb   = dot_ncb (k, t);
    let ncrb  = dot_nc_rb (k, t);
    let rows  = dot_rows (k, t);
    let rowsk = dot_rows_k (k, t);
    let rb    = kernel_rb (k, t);
    let i     = core.idx 0 6 (pick_rule @5 (k#packed,
                    dot_static (k, t) (core.bit2.and_ 2 (ncb != 0, rb (rows, ncrb))),
                    dot_static (k, t) (core.bit2.and_ 2 (ncb != 0, rb (rowsk, ncb))),
                    dot_static (k, t) (ncb != 0),
                    (dot_static (k, t) (core.bit2.and_ 2 (kernel_ns (k, t) + kernel_ni (k, t) + 1 == t#Ro, rb (rows, kernel_n t))), ff)#(k#plain)));
    ((0, ncrb, ncb, ncb, 0, 0)#i, (rows, rows, rowsk, 0, rows, 0)#i, (tt, ff, tt, tt, ff, ff)#i);
/// The outermost loop goes to the runtime's threads when `target.threads` has several and it has at least two
/// iterations per thread, and the schedule is the plain register-blocked one with no peeled remainder anywhere:
/// measured against an executable, scalar-reference-checked test under real threads, every one of the other shapes
/// `dot_trace` picks races or miscompiles instead of just costing an opportunity, so this stays deliberately narrow
/// rather than risk a false positive — a case excluded here that is in fact safe is a missed speedup, nothing worse.
/// Excluded: a packed kernel's `cache_read` panel (`dot_product_impl`) is a single buffer staged (`compute_at`) once
/// per outer iteration and reused across the rest; `dot_kblk`'s own precondition for `hoist_red` to hoist an
/// accumulator live across the same outer iterations for the same reason (mirrored here from `kq`/`ts`/`nc`, since
/// `dot_kblk` itself runs on the traced schedule, where that no longer folds — see below); an indivisible K forces
/// `dot_k`'s own tile into a peeled tail (`partition_red`) whose closure-converted body does not compile (`clos`'s
/// `mem.seo` does not reach a fixed point) under `mem.par_for`; an indivisible N similarly needs `ptail`
/// (`partition_par`) to skip the excluded points of the outer loop's own last block, which miscomputes under
/// threads; and `rows == 0` falls back to `dot_schedule`'s plain vector loop, whose outermost dim is `M`, not the
/// `N / 16` this reads.
/// All of this is read off the *untraced* kernel and tile (`kernel_k`, `kernel_n`, `nc`, `rows`) instead of the
/// traced schedule's own domain: by the time `dot_trace` has strip-mined and interchanged dim 0 into place, its
/// extent no longer folds to a literal here (dot_params's own picks, read before any of that, do).
lam dot_par (k: Kernel, t0: Tileable, nc rows: Nat, packed kblk: Bool) (t: Tileable): Tileable =
    let th      = target.threads ();
    let n0      = kernel_n t0 / 16;
    let K       = kernel_k t0;
    let k_tail  = core.bit2.and_ 2 (4 <= K, core.bit2.and_ 2 (K % 4 != 0, K % 3 != 0));
    let n_tail  = kernel_n t0 % 16 != 0;
    // dot_tile/dot_kblk's own tile and cache-block check, replayed on the untraced K.
    let ts_tile = ((1, 3)#(K % 3 == 0), 4)#(K % 4 == 0);
    let kq      = K / ts_tile;
    let kblk_hz = core.bit2.and_ 2 (kblk, target.cache_elems () < kq * ts_tile * nc);
    let safe    = core.bit2.and_ 2 ((tt, ff)#packed,
                  core.bit2.and_ 2 ((tt, ff)#kblk_hz,
                  core.bit2.and_ 2 ((tt, ff)#k_tail,
                  core.bit2.and_ 2 ((tt, ff)#n_tail, (ff, tt)#(rows != 0)))));
    let ok      = core.bit2.and_ 2 (safe, core.bit2.and_ 2 (1 < th, 2 * th <= n0));
    parallelize (t, (t#par, 0)#(if_static (n0, ok, ff)));
/// Two-dimensional parallel GEMM uses disjoint output tiles and the register-blocked nest's local SSA
/// accumulators. Packed operands are materialized before the parallel call; reduction-outer scratch panels are
/// unused. Keep tails on the existing schedule until their partitioned iteration domains can be fused too.
lam dot_parallel_ok (m n k: Nat): Bool =
    if_static (m * n * k, core.bit2.and_ 2 (1 < target.threads (),
        core.bit2.and_ 2 (1048576 <= m * n * k,
        core.bit2.and_ 2 (8 <= m, core.bit2.and_ 2 (32 <= n,
        core.bit2.and_ 2 (m % 4 == 0, core.bit2.and_ 2 (n % 16 == 0,
        core.bit2.or_ 2 (k % 4 == 0, k % 3 == 0))))))), ff);
lam dot_parallel_kernel (k: Kernel, t: Tileable): Bool =
    let n = (kernel_n t,
             t#So#(core.idx 0 (t#Ro) (t#Ro - 2)) * kernel_n t)#(k#packed);
    core.bit2.and_ 2 (core.bit2.and_ 2 ((tt, ff)#(k#plain), core.bit2.or_ 2 ((tt, ff)#(k#packed), k#shared)),
        core.bit2.and_ 2 (core.bit2.and_ 2 (t#Ro == t#Rp, t#Ro == (2, 3)#(k#packed)), core.bit2.and_ 2 (t#Rr == 1,
        core.bit2.and_ 2 (kernel_ns (k, t) == 0, core.bit2.and_ 2 (kernel_ni (k, t) == 1,
        dot_parallel_ok (kernel_m (k, t), n, kernel_k t))))));
/// Largest power-of-two divisor not exceeding the micro-kernel blocks per output tile.
lam dot_parallel_tile (n cap: Nat): Nat =
    lam fits (v: Nat): Bool = core.bit2.and_ 2 (v <= cap, n % v == 0);
    ((((1, 2)#(fits 2), 4)#(fits 4), 8)#(fits 8), 16)#(fits 16);
lam dot_parallel (t: Tileable, packed: Bool): Tileable =
    lam plain_rb (u: []): Tileable = dot_schedule_rb (2, 4) t;
    lam packed_rb (u: []): Tileable =
        let rb = strip_mine_par (t, 0, 4);
        let r = rb#2_15 + rb#3_15;
        let perm = ‹j: r; let jj = core.bitcast Nat j;
                         core.select (jj == 0, 2, core.select (jj == 1, 0, core.select (jj == 2, 1, jj)))›;
        dot_k (vectorize (interchange (rb, perm), 2));
    let rb = (plain_rb, packed_rb)#packed ();
    let n = rb#Sr#(core.idx 0 (rb#Rp + rb#Rr) 0);
    let m = t#So#(core.idx 0 (t#Ro) 0) / 4;
    let nt0 = dot_parallel_tile (n, 8);
    let mt0 = dot_parallel_tile (m, 16);
    let nt = dot_parallel_tile (n, (8, 2)#(n / nt0 * (m / mt0) < target.threads ()));
    let mt = dot_parallel_tile (m, (16, 4)#(n / nt * (m / mt0) < target.threads ()));
    let nb = strip_mine_par (rb, 0, nt);
    let mb = strip_mine_par (nb, 2, mt);
    let r = mb#2_15 + mb#3_15;
    let perm = ‹j: r; let jj = core.bitcast Nat j;
                     core.select (jj == 1, 2, core.select (jj == 2, 1, jj))›;
    parallelize (fuse_par (interchange (mb, perm), 0), 0);
lam dot_rule (k: Kernel, t: Tileable): Tileable =
    let (nc, rows, kblk) = dot_params (k, t);
    lam serial (u: []): Tileable =
        dot_par (k, t, nc, rows, k#packed, kblk) (dot_trace (nc, rows, kblk, k#packed) (t#Ro, kernel_ns (k, t), kernel_ni (k, t)) t);
    lam parallel (u: []): Tileable = dot_parallel (t, k#packed);
    (serial, parallel)#(dot_parallel_kernel (k, t)) ();
lam dot_rule_rows (k: Kernel, t: Tileable): Nat = ((dot_params (k, t))#1_3, 4)#(dot_parallel_kernel (k, t));
lam dot_rule_any (k: Kernel, t: Tileable): Bool = tt;
///
/// #### Windowed kernels
///
/// A kernel whose left operand's unit-stride axis is driven by several loop dims — the output's last dim and the last
/// reduction dim, at the strides `conv_schedule` decides by; tried first, since its right operand's unit-stride axis is
/// a reduction dim as in x·Wᵀ.
lam conv_rule (k: Kernel, t: Tileable): Tileable =
    conv_schedule (k#unit (0, t#Ro - 1), k#unit (0, t#Rp + t#Rr - 1)) t;
lam conv_rule_ok (k: Kernel, t: Tileable): Bool = kernel_windowed (k, t);
///
/// The rules, in the order they are tried; every other kernel is an A·B contraction.
let sched_rules = ((conv_rule_ok,           dot_rows_none, conv_rule),
                   (dot_rule_kvec_plain_ok, dot_rows_none, dot_rule_kvec_plain),
                   (dot_rule_kvec_ok,       dot_rows_none, dot_rule_kvec));
let dot_fallback = (dot_rule_any, dot_rule_rows, dot_rule);
///
/// ### conv_impl

priv anx lam conv_impl (R: Ring) {n cin cout h w kh kw: Nat}
                      (stride dilation padding: «2; Nat»)
                      (input: «n, cin, h, w; R#T», weight: «cout, cin, kh, kw; R#T»)
                    : «conv_shape (n, cout, (h, w), (kh, kw), stride, dilation, padding); R#T» =
    // Zero-pad only the spatial axes (lo = hi = (0, 0, pad_h, pad_w)); the pad value is the ring zero.
    let s_in     = (n, cin, h, w);
    let pad_lohi = (0, 0, padding#0_2, padding#1_2);
    let padded   = pad s_in (0, pad_lohi, pad_lohi) (input, R#_0);
    let s_padded = shape 4 padded;
    let s_out    = conv_shape (n, cout, (h, w), (kh, kw), stride, dilation, padding);
    let oh       = s_out#2_4;
    let ow       = s_out#3_4;

    // The SCHEDULE, selected in the frontend from the parameters (see `conv_schedule`): unroll the
    // kh·kw window into the loop body (constant tap offsets) when it fits the budget, and run `ow`
    // as the innermost, unit-stride loop — for a fixed tap, adjacent output pixels read adjacent
    // input pixels, so the vectorizer emits contiguous vector loads instead of gathers. The
    // schedule may strip-mine `oh` (row blocking), packing the materialized tensor along that axis;
    // the row-major `reshape` restores `s_out` (an identity for the neutral split, and absorbed into
    // the consumers' access maps by `fuse_tensor`'s read-through in a fused graph).
    let t = mr_tileable @2 @(4, 3)
            ((n, cout, oh, ow), (0, 1, 2, 3), (n, cout, oh, ow, cin, kh, kw))
            @((R#T, R#T), (4, 4), (s_padded, (cout, cin, kh, kw)))
            (conv_fun R, R#_0, id @(R#T))
            (proj_map @(7, 4) (0, 1, 2, 3))
            (conv2d_in_map (stride, dilation), proj_map @(7, 4) (1, 4, 5, 6))
            (padded, weight);
    // The kernel's structure (see `Kernel`): the input reads every dim but cout, its w axis at stride_w · ow +
    // dilation_w · kw; the weight reads cout and the window, kw unit-stride.
    lam reads (i d: Nat): Bool = ((tt, ff)#(d == 1), core.bit2.or_ 2 (d == 1, 4 <= d))#(i == 1);
    lam unit (i d: Nat): Nat =
        (core.select (d == 3, stride#1_2, core.select (d == 6, dilation#1_2, 0)), (0, 1)#(d == 6))#(i == 1);
    schedule_kernel @(R#T) (sched_rules, dot_fallback) (s_out, 0) (reads, unit, ff, ff, ff) t;
///
/// ### dot_general_fun
///
/// The fold step: a fused multiply-add in the ring `R`.
pub fun dot_general_fun (R: Ring) (x: R#T, (y z: R#T))@tt: R#T = return (R#add (x, (R#mul (y, z))));
///
/// ### dot_general_pick
///
/// Sends input axis `i` to the loop id it reads — batching dims first, then the contractions, then
/// the remaining free axes.
pub lam dot_general_pick {n: Nat} {na nb nc: Nat} (a: «na; Idx n», b: «nb; Idx n», c: «nc; Idx n») (off_1 off_2: Nat) (i: Idx n): Nat =
  match vec.first core.icmp.e (b, i) with
      | bi: Idx nb => core.bitcast Nat bi
      | _:  []     => match vec.first core.icmp.e (c, i) with
          | ci: Idx nc => off_2 + core.bitcast Nat ci
          | _:  []     => match vec.first core.icmp.e (a, i) with
              | ai: Idx na => off_1 + core.bitcast Nat ai
              | _:  []     => 0;
///
/// ### dot_product_impl
///
/// `r1`/`r2` are an *explicit* group here, unlike in the `dot_product` axiom: they must be literals
/// already when this `lam` is β-reduced, since the subscript rows are built with `vec.diff`/`vec.fold.l`,
/// whose normalizers decline to fold over a non-literal rank, and nothing re-normalizes the body once a
/// Hole is resolved later.
/// `{s1 s2}` has no such problem and stays implicit.
///
/// `tensor::Lower` re-applies the axiom's groups positionally, so the two curry shapes still match —
/// except for the leading `fastest_2`, which has no axiom counterpart: it is `fastest_axis` of the
/// right operand, pre-applied by `Lower` where that operand is concrete.
/// The schedule **decision** built on it lives in the body:
///
/// - a contracted fastest axis (\f$x \cdot W^T\f$, e.g. behind a weight transpose the read-through absorbs) selects
///   `dot_schedule_kvec`, anything else `dot_schedule`;
/// - the selected schedule may strip-mine an output dim (row/output-cell blocking), packing the
///   materialized tensor along that axis, whereupon the row-major `reshape` restores `s_out` — the
///   identity for the neutral split, and absorbed into the consumers' access maps by `fuse_tensor`'s
///   read-through in a fused graph.

lam dot_contract
  (fastest_2: Nat) (plain: Bool) (packed shared: Bool) {r_fin: Nat} (s_fin: «r_fin; Nat»)
  (R: Ring) (r1 r2: Nat) {nc nb: Nat}
  (c1: «nc; Idx r1», c2: «nc; Idx r2», b1: «nb; Idx r1», b2: «nb; Idx r2»)
  {s1: «r1; Nat», s2: «r2; Nat»} (a: «s1; R#T», b: «s2; R#T»)
    : «s_fin; R#T»
    = let s_out    = dot_general_shape (c1, c2, b1, b2) (s1, s2);
      let bc_1     = vec.cat (b1, c1);
      let s1_res   = vec.diff (s1, bc_1);
      let n_s1_res = vec.len s1_res;
      let bc_2     = vec.cat (b2, c2);
      let f        = dot_general_fun R;
      let r_out    = vec.len s_out;
      let a1       = vec.diff (‹i: r1; i›, bc_1);
      let a2       = vec.diff (‹i: r2; i›, bc_2);
      let subs_1   = ‹i: r1; dot_general_pick (a1, b1, c1) (nb, r_out) i›;
      let subs_2   = ‹i: r2; dot_general_pick (a2, b2, c2) (nb + n_s1_res, r_out) i›;
      // The subscript rows use consecutive loop ids: 0 … r_out−1 are the output loops
      // and r_out … r_out+nc−1 the contractions, whose bounds are the contracting extents of `a`.
      let s_red    = ‹i: nc; s1#(c1#i)›;
      let s_full   = vec.cat (s_out, s_red);
      let n_loops  = r_out + nc;
      let t        = mr_tileable @2 @(r_out, nc)
                         (s_out, ‹i: r_out; core.bitcast Nat i›, s_full)
                         @((R#T, R#T), (r1, r2), (s1, s2))
                         (f, R#_0, id @(R#T))
                         (proj_map @(n_loops, r_out) ‹i: r_out; core.bitcast Nat i›)
                         (proj_map @(n_loops, r1) subs_1, proj_map @(n_loops, r2) subs_2)
                         (a, b);
      // The kernel's structure (see `Kernel`): input `i` reads loop dim `d` where `d` is among its subscripts; the left
      // operand's unit-stride axis is its last, the right one's `fastest_2`.
      lam reads (i d: Nat): Bool =
          option.is_some (vec.first `== ((subs_1, subs_2)#(i == 1), d));
      lam unit (i d: Nat): Nat =
          let f2 = core.select (fastest_2 < r2, fastest_2, 0);
          ((0, 1)#(subs_1#(core.idx 0 r1 (r1 - 1)) == d),
           (0, 1)#(core.bit2.and_ 2 (fastest_2 < r2, subs_2#(core.idx 0 r2 f2) == d)))#(i == 1);
      let a_m = (0, nb + n_s1_res - 1)#(1 <= n_s1_res);
      schedule_kernel @(R#T) (sched_rules, dot_fallback) (s_fin, a_m) (reads, unit, plain, packed, shared) t;
///
/// ### cache_read
///
/// TVM's `cache_read` with a packed layout for an operand of a projection-indexed op: axis `a` of `x`, split by `ts`,
/// becomes the block axis `⌈s#a/ts⌉` at position `p` of the result and the lane axis `ts` its last — BLAS's packed
/// panels, e.g. «K, N» into «N/16, K, 16» for `(a, ts, p) = (1, 16, 0)`.
/// A consumer whose loops already split the axis reads the result as a projection (see `cache_read_axis`); the copy is
/// `compute_at` its `level` leading parallel loops, one block at a time.
/// Where `ts` does not divide the axis, the last block's missing lanes wrap around to the start of the axis
/// (`cache_read_map`): in bounds, and finite values that only reach the consumer's padded cells, which it discards.
///
/// #### cache_read_axis
///
/// The axis of the result that holds an axis `j ≠ a` of the operand; the block axis is `p`, the lane axis `r`.
lam cache_read_axis (r a p: Nat) (j: Nat): Nat =
    let j1 = (j, j - 1)#(a < j);
    (j1, j1 + 1)#(p <= j1);
///
/// #### cache_read_shape
lam cache_read_shape {r: Nat} (s: «r; Nat», a ts p: Nat): «r + 1; Nat» =
    ‹j: r + 1; let jj = core.bitcast Nat j;
               core.select (jj == p, (s#(core.idx 0 r a) + ts - 1) / ts,
               core.select (jj == r, ts,
               s#(core.idx 0 r (let jj1 = (jj, jj - 1)#(p < jj); (jj1, jj1 + 1)#(a <= jj1)))))›;
///
/// #### cache_read_map
///
/// The read of the operand at a coordinate of the result: axis `a` of extent `n` at `block · ts + lane`, modulo `n`
/// where `ts` does not divide it.
lam cache_read_map (r a ts p n: Nat) (o: «r + 1; affine.Index»): «r; affine.Index» =
    ‹j: r; let jj  = core.bitcast Nat j;
           let col = affine.op.add (affine.semiop.mul (o#(core.idx 0 (r + 1) p), ts), o#(core.idx 0 (r + 1) r));
           core.select (jj == a, (affine.semiop.rem (col, n), col)#(n / ts * ts == n),
                        o#(core.idx 0 (r + 1) (cache_read_axis (r, a, p) jj)))›;
///
/// #### cache_read
lam cache_read {T: *, r: Nat} {s: «r; Nat»} (x: «s; T») (a ts p level: Nat): «cache_read_shape (s, a, ts, p); T» =
    let n    = s#(core.idx 0 r a);
    let s_pk = cache_read_shape (s, a, ts, p);
    compute_at level
        (map_reduce @1 @(T, r + 1, r + 1) (s_pk, s_pk) (tensor_copy, ⊥: T) affine.id (cache_read_map (r, a, ts, p, n),) x);
///
/// ### dot_product_impl
///
/// Packs the right operand of a register-blocked contraction of its second-to-last axis K into panels the width of a
/// register block (see `dot_pack_nc`, `cache_read`): «…, K, N» becomes «…, ⌈N/16⌉, K, 16», so the rows of B a block
/// reads are adjacent cache lines instead of N elements apart, `compute_at` the panel loop.
/// Where 16 does not divide N, the result's «…, N» prefix is copied out of the padded one (`dot_contract`'s `unpack`).
/// The contraction then takes ⌈N/16⌉ as a free dim of its own (`dot_contract` with `packed`), and its «…, ⌈N/16⌉, 16»
/// result is the row-major «…, N».
priv anx lam dot_product_impl
  (fastest_2: Nat) (plain: Bool)
  (R: Ring) (r1 r2: Nat) {nc nb: Nat}
  (c1: «nc; Idx r1», c2: «nc; Idx r2», b1: «nb; Idx r1», b2: «nb; Idx r2»)
  {s1: «r1; Nat», s2: «r2; Nat»} (a: «s1; R#T», b: «s2; R#T»)
    : let s_out = dot_general_shape (c1, c2, b1, b2) (s1, s2);
      «s_out; R#T»
    = let s_out = dot_general_shape (c1, c2, b1, b2) (s1, s2);
      let r_out = vec.len s_out;
      let k     = s2#(core.idx 0 r2 (r2 - 2));
      let n     = s2#(core.idx 0 r2 (r2 - 1));
      let s1_res = vec.diff (s1, vec.cat (b1, c1));
      let m      = vec.fold.r @(Nat, Nat) @(vec.len s1_res) (lm (x acc: Nat): Nat = x * acc) (s1_res, 1);
      let n_res  = vec.len s1_res;
      let m_rb   = (0, s_out#(core.idx 0 r_out ((0, nb + n_res - 1)#(1 <= n_res))))#(1 <= n_res);
      let pc    = dot_pack_nc (m, m_rb, k, n, core.bit2.and_ 2 (nb == 0, n_res == 1));
      // One contraction, of b's second-to-last axis; N a free axis of b and the last of the result.
      let shape_ok = core.bit2.and_ 2 (nc == 1, core.bit2.and_ 2 (2 <= r2, core.bit2.and_ 2 (2 <= r_out,
                     core.bit2.and_ 2 (core.bitcast Nat (c2#(core.idx 0 nc 0)) + 2 == r2,
                     core.bit2.and_ 2 (fastest_2 + 1 == r2, (tt, ff)#(option.is_some (vec.first `== (‹i: nb; core.bitcast Nat (b2#i)›, r2 - 1))))))));
      let par = core.bit2.and_ 2 (r1 == 2, core.bit2.and_ 2 (r2 == 2,
                  core.bit2.and_ 2 (shape_ok, core.bit2.and_ 2 (nb == 0, dot_parallel_ok (m, n, k)))));
      let pack  = (if_static (pc * r2, core.bit2.and_ 2 (shape_ok, pc != 0), ff), ff)#plain;
      // The panels «…, ⌈N/16⌉, K, 16»: the block axis after b's batch dims, K and the lanes behind it.
      let s_pk  = cache_read_shape (s2, r2 - 1, pc, r2 - 2);
      let c2_pk = ‹i: nc; core.idx 0 (r2 + 1) (cache_read_axis (r2, r2 - 1, r2 - 2) (r2 - 2))›;
      let b2_pk = ‹i: nb; core.idx 0 (r2 + 1) (cache_read_axis (r2, r2 - 1, r2 - 2) (core.bitcast Nat (b2#i)))›;
      lam go_plain (u: []): «s_out; R#T» = dot_contract fastest_2 plain (ff, ff) s_out R (r1, r2) (c1, c2, b1, b2) (a, b);
      // Packed per N block: the panel is computed in the contraction's nest, below its outer free dims of b.
      lam go_pack (u: []): «s_out; R#T» =
          lam staged (u: []): «s_pk; R#T» = cache_read b (r2 - 1, pc, r2 - 2, r_out - vec.len s1_res);
          // Parallel consumers share a fully materialized, immutable operand; no worker refills a shared tile.
          lam shared (u: []): «s_pk; R#T» =
              compute_at 0 (map_reduce @1 @(R#T, r2 + 1, r2 + 1) (s_pk, s_pk) (tensor_copy, ⊥: (R#T))
                  affine.id (cache_read_map (r2, r2 - 1, pc, r2 - 2, n),) b);
          let bp = (staged, shared)#par ();
          dot_contract r2 ff (tt, par) s_out R (r1, r2 + 1) (c1, c2_pk, b1, b2_pk) @(s1, s_pk) (a, bp);
      (go_plain, go_pack)#pack ();
///
/// ### bmm_impl
///
/// The `{r1 r2}`/`{s1 s2}` groups are explicit for the same reason as in `product_2d_impl`.
priv anx lam bmm_impl (fastest_2: Nat) (plain: Bool) (R: Ring) {B M K N: Nat}
                      (t1: «B, M, K; R#T», t2: «B, K, N; R#T»): «B, M, N; R#T»
        = dot_product_impl fastest_2 plain R (3, 3) (2_3, 1_3, 0_3, 0_3) (t1, t2);
///
/// ### product_2d_impl
///
/// The `{r1 r2}` and `{s1 s2}` groups are passed explicitly — not because inference fails, but because
/// of *when* it succeeds: `dot_product_impl` is a curried `lam`, so applying it to a group β-reduces
/// the body right away.
/// With the ranks still unsolved Holes the `vec.diff`/`vec.fold.l` subscript machinery is built over
/// non-literals, its normalizers decline to fold, and nothing re-normalizes the body once the Hole%s
/// are resolved: the result type-checks but carries the whole `vec.*` scaffolding inside its access
/// maps (`lit/tensor/dot_product.mim` pins this down).
/// Passing literals up front folds it away.
priv anx lam product_2d_impl (fastest_2: Nat) (plain: Bool) (R: Ring) {m k l: Nat}
                             (t1: «m, k; R#T», t2: «k, l; R#T»): «m, l; R#T»
        = dot_product_impl fastest_2 plain R (2, 2) (1_2, 0_2, (), ()) (t1, t2);
///
/// ### scatter_step_impl
///
/// One update: reads `index` and `updates` at the visited coordinate and inserts the element into the
/// accumulated tensor at the destination it selects.
priv anx lam scatter_step_impl {T: *, r: Nat}
                          (s_src: «r; Nat», s_idx: «r; Nat», s_updates: «r; Nat»)
                          (dim: Idx r)
                          (visit_indices: «r; I64»)
                          (acc: «s_src; T», index: «s_idx; Idx (s_src#dim)», updates: «s_updates; T»): «s_src; T» =
    let index_coords = ‹d: r; core.conv.u (s_idx#d) (visit_indices#d)›;
    let update_coords = ‹d: r; core.conv.u (s_updates#d) (visit_indices#d)›;
    let destination_axis_index = core.bitcast I64 (index#index_coords);
    let update = updates#update_coords;
    let destination_coords = ‹d: r;
        let coordinate = core.select (core.icmp.e @r (d, dim), destination_axis_index, visit_indices#d);
        core.conv.u (s_src#d) coordinate›;
    acc#destination_coords ← update;
///
/// ### slice_map
///
/// `o ↦ (start#d + step#d · o#d)_d`: the strided read map.
lam slice_map {r: Nat} (start step: «r; Nat») (o: «r; affine.Index»): «r; affine.Index» =
    ‹d: r; affine.op.add (affine.semiop.mul (o#d, step#d), affine.lit (start#d))›;
///
/// ### slice_impl

priv anx lam slice_impl {T: *, r: Nat} (s_in: «r; Nat»)
                       (start step s_out: «r; Nat») (input: «s_in; T»): «s_out; T» =
    map_reduce (s_out, s_out) (tensor_copy, ⊥: T)
        affine.id (slice_map (start, step),) input;
///
/// ### transpose_map
///
/// `o ↦ (o#(perm#0), …, o#(perm#(r−1)))`: input axis `j` reads output-loop var `o#(perm#j)`.
lam transpose_map {r: Nat} (perm: «r; Idx r») (o: «r; affine.Index»): «r; affine.Index» = ‹j: r; o#(perm#j)›;
///
/// ### transpose_impl

priv anx lam transpose_impl {T: *, r: Nat}
                           (permutation: «r; Idx r») {s: «r; Nat»} (input: «s; T»)
                         : «transpose_shape (s, permutation); T»
                         = let out_s = transpose_shape (s, permutation);
                           map_reduce (out_s, out_s) (tensor_copy, ⊥: T)
                               affine.id (transpose_map permutation,) input;
///
/// ### binary_impl
///
/// `map_impl` over two tensors.
priv anx lam binary_impl {Ti1 Ti2 To: *} (app: [Ti1, Ti2] → To) {r: Nat, s: «r; Nat»} (is: [«s; Ti1», «s; Ti2»]): «s; To» =
  map_impl app is;
///
/// ### select_impl
///
/// `map_impl` of `core.select` over three tensors.
priv anx lam select_impl {T: *} {r: Nat, s: «r; Nat»} (is: [«s; Bool», «s; T», «s; T»]): «s; T» =
  map_impl core.select is;
///
/// ### unary_impl
///
/// `map_impl` over one tensor.
priv anx lam unary_impl {Ti To : *} (app: Ti → To) {r: Nat, s: «r; Nat»} (i: «s; Ti»): «s; To» =
  map_impl app i;
///
/// ## Phases
///
/// ### reassoc
///
/// Reassociates chains of `product_2d` with the classic matrix-chain-order dynamic program — but
/// minimizing vector-lane slots instead of scalar multiplications.
///
/// - The vector loop runs over a product's trailing extent (`dot_schedule`) or, for a transposed
///   operand, over its contraction (`dot_schedule_kvec`), and a literal extent is charged rounded up
///   to whole lanes — `-X tensor:reassoc-vec=<n>`, `1` to count plain scalar multiplications.
///   A bracketing whose intermediates are too narrow to fill a vector thus pays for the lanes it
///   leaves idle, which is exactly what the multiplication count misses.
/// - Extents need not be literal: a cost is a polynomial in the symbolic extents, ordered by
///   coefficient-wise `≤`, which proves `≤` for every instantiation because extents are non-negative.
/// - That order is only *partial*, so a chain can have several bracketings that each win for some
///   instantiation — a batch dimension favouring a different one when small than when large.
///   Up to 4 matrices — `-X tensor:reassoc-max=<n>`, below `3` to switch it off — those survivors are
///   emitted side by side behind a run-time comparison of their costs, so only the winner's thunk
///   runs; a longer chain is only reassociated where one bracketing provably wins for every extent.
/// - Common sub-products are hash-consed across the alternatives, so the code growth stays well below
///   \f$\mathit{Catalan}(n-1)\f$.
/// - A product with more than one consumer stays a chain boundary, as pulling it apart would leave it
///   materialized for its other consumers as well.
/// - A transposed product is part of the chain, as `(A · B)ᵀ = Bᵀ · Aᵀ`; so `Q · Kᵀ` with `Q = x · wq` and
///   `K = x · wk` is the chain `x · wq · wkᵀ · xᵀ`.
/// - Reassociation is only exact for an associative ring; on floating-point rings it changes the
///   rounding, just like `-ffast-math`.
///
axm reassoc: compile.Phase;
///
/// ### lower_tensor
///
/// Lowers the derived operations to the core ones by re-applying the matching `_impl` annexes.
///
axm lower_tensor: compile.Phase;
///
/// ### lower_map_reduce
///
/// Lowers the core operations, `generate` included, to their underlying primitives: loops, `extract`,
/// `insert`, packs, ...
///
axm lower_map_reduce: compile.Phase;
///
/// ### fuse_tensor
///
/// Fuses pure producer `map_reduce`s into their consumers' access maps.
///
/// - A producer is only fused where the consumer reads it injectively — its access map uses every
///   loop index, possibly strided/shifted — so fusion never recomputes a producer element; strided
///   reads even skip producer elements entirely.
/// - In the reverse direction, a trailing elementwise map over a reducing `map_reduce` is composed
///   into the producer's `post` epilogue (the GEMM-epilogue pattern), provided the map is its only
///   consumer.
///
axm fuse_tensor: compile.Phase;
///
/// ### lower_to_mem
///
/// Bufferizes the core operations onto the `buffer` layer: tensor values become `buffer.Buf` handles,
/// `extract` / `insert` / `generate` / `map_reduce` become `buffer.read` / `buffer.write` / `buffer.alloc`
/// threading `mem.M`.
/// This is where a `buf` annotation is consumed: it seeds the set of array types that denote tensors, next to
/// the operand and result types of the operations above.
/// `mem.add_mem` (scheduled next in the pipeline) threads the memory; run `buffer.lower_ptr`
/// afterwards to reach `mem.Ptr`.
///
axm lower_to_mem: compile.Phase;
